const VERSION_C={"version":"C","slides":{"2:1":{"title":"Describing a population, then learning from a sample","paragraphs":["A population is the full group of interest. Its center describes a typical measurement; its dispersion describes how much the measurements differ. A Normal distribution adds a description of the overall shape.","Measuring everyone is usually impractical, so a sample provides observations from the population. The sample mean estimates the population mean, while the standard error describes how much that estimated mean would move across repeated samples.","The kind of measurement also matters. Height and blood pressure are numerical measurements, whereas categories label groups. The class survey connects these ideas to real student measurements before group comparisons."]},"2:2":{"title":"R and RStudio: the tools around the data","paragraphs":["R performs statistical calculations. RStudio provides a workspace around R, including a place to write commands, inspect objects, view plots, and read help. Installing the interface and installing the calculation engine are separate tasks.","A working directory is the folder R uses to find input files and save output. Variables hold objects; vectors collect values; matrices and arrays arrange values into structured sets.","A data frame organizes observations into rows and variables into columns. Packages add functions, and loading a package makes its functions available during a session. These are tools for organizing the measurements for analysis."]},"2:3":{"title":"Population mean and population spread","paragraphs":["Each dot represents an individual's height. The Martian population is centered at 40 cm and the Venusian population at 15 cm. Those centers are the population means, written \\(\\mu\\) (mu).","To obtain a mean, add all heights and divide by \\(N\\) (the number of individuals in the population). To describe spread, subtract the mean from each height, square those distances, and average the squares.","That average squared distance is \\(\\sigma^2\\) (population variance). Its square root, \\(\\sigma\\) (population standard deviation), returns spread to centimeters. The Martian heights have a larger absolute spread as well as a larger mean."]},"2:4":{"title":"What a Normal population looks like","paragraphs":["A Normal distribution describes a symmetric, bell-shaped pattern of measurements. Measurements near the center are common, and measurements far from the center become less common in either direction.","The mean \\(\\mu\\) locates the center. The standard deviation \\(\\sigma\\) controls the width: a larger standard deviation spreads measurements farther from the mean without necessarily moving that mean.","Several inference methods in this course assume Normal populations. For now, connect the shape to these two quantities; probability calculations later turn areas under this curve into precise proportions of individuals."]},"2:5":{"title":"Reading positions on a Normal curve","paragraphs":["Start at the mean: half the population lies below it and half above it. Moving one standard deviation to the right includes about 84% of the population below that cutoff; moving one to the left leaves about 16% below it.","The interval from one standard deviation below the mean to one above contains about 68% of measurements. Widening the interval to two standard deviations captures about 95%, and three captures nearly all of them.","A cutoff and an interval ask different questions. A cutoff includes everything to its left; an interval includes only the area between two boundaries. Draw those boundaries before deciding which proportion is needed."]},"2:6":{"title":"Symmetry, skewness, and tails","paragraphs":["A Normal distribution is symmetric, but real measurements may form a lopsided pattern. Skewness describes that lack of symmetry: one side of the distribution extends differently from the other.","Kurtosis concerns the tails relative to a Normal distribution. Heavy tails put more probability into unusually distant measurements; light tails put less there.","Two data sets can have similar centers and spreads while having different shapes. Look at the distribution itself as well as its mean and standard deviation, especially when a later test assumes a Normal population."]},"2:7":{"title":"Estimating population quantities from a sample","paragraphs":["A sample contains \\(n\\) measured individuals from a larger population. Its mean, \\(\\bar{x}\\) (x-bar), is the total of those measurements divided by the number sampled.","Sample variance measures squared distances from that sample mean. Divide the sum of those squared distances by \\(n-1\\), then take the square root if you want the sample standard deviation \\(s\\).","Random sampling aims to avoid systematically favoring certain individuals. The sample's center and spread estimate the population's center and spread, extending the description beyond the measured individuals."]},"2:8":{"title":"Median, range, and quartiles","paragraphs":["Put the observations in ascending order first. The median is the middle observation when the sample size is odd; when it is even, average the two middle observations.","The smallest and largest observations show the endpoints of the data. Percentiles locate positions within the ordered list, so the 25th and 75th percentiles identify the lower and upper boundaries of the middle half.","These summaries answer different questions from the mean and variance. The median identifies a central position in the ordered data, while percentile ranges describe how widely a specified portion of the data is spread."]},"2:9":{"title":"Finding a percentile between observations","paragraphs":["A percentile may fall between two positions in the sorted data. The course's rank rule first locates that position using the requested percentile and the sample size.","The integer part of the rank identifies the lower observation. The fractional part tells you how far to move toward the next observation: a fraction near zero stays close to the lower value, while a fraction near one approaches the upper value.","Interpolation means taking that fraction of the gap and adding it to the lower observation. Keep the rank, the two selected data values, and the fractional distance separate so their roles stay clear."]},"2:10":{"title":"The 30th percentile of five measurements","paragraphs":["The ordered measurements are 3, 7, 8, 9, and 14. A rank of 2.2 means the requested percentile sits between the second observation, 7, and the third observation, 8.","The decimal part is 0.2, so move one fifth of the way across the gap from 7 to 8. The gap is one unit, making the interpolated percentile 7.2.","The rank is a position, not the answer's measurement value. Reading 2.2 as the percentile itself would confuse the location in the list with the value found at that location."]},"2:11":{"title":"Histograms and the middle half of the data","paragraphs":["A histogram groups nearby measurements into bins and counts how many fall in each bin. Taller bars indicate more observations in that measurement range, allowing the overall shape to become visible.","Quartiles divide the ordered data into four parts. The second quartile is the median, and the first and third quartiles bound the middle half of the observations.","The blood-pressure example subtracts the first quartile from the third to get an interquartile range of 16 mmHg. That describes the width of the central half, rather than the distance between the two most extreme measurements."]},"2:12":{"title":"Reading a box plot","paragraphs":["The box extends from the first quartile to the third quartile, with a mark for the median inside. Its width along the measurement scale represents the interquartile range.","The whiskers extend outward according to the displayed convention, using boundaries based on 1.5 times the interquartile range and the available extremes. Observations beyond those boundaries may appear separately.","Read the median, box, and whiskers as different pieces of information. A long upper side suggests more extension toward large values, while a short box indicates that the middle half is tightly clustered."]},"2:13":{"title":"Reading a violin plot","paragraphs":["A violin plot adds information about the distribution's shape to the quartile summary. Wider regions indicate measurement ranges where values are more concentrated; narrower regions indicate fewer values.","The central marks still locate the median and quartiles. The adjacent values use the interquartile-range convention shown alongside the plot, so the distribution outline and the central summary serve different purposes.","Follow the measurement axis to compare values, then look across the violin to compare concentration. This lets you see whether a broad-looking data set is evenly spread or contains a particularly dense cluster."]},"2:14":{"title":"A first check for a Normal pattern","paragraphs":["Use the sample mean and sample standard deviation to mark positions on the data scale. If the observations resemble a Normal population, about two thirds should fall within one sample standard deviation of the sample mean.","About 95% should fall within two standard deviations. You can also count observations below a boundary and compare that proportion with the expected left-tail proportion.","This connects an observed data set to the familiar bell-curve pattern. The sample size affects how closely the observed proportions match; later Normal quantile plots provide another way to compare the whole pattern."]},"2:15":{"title":"Why different samples give different answers","paragraphs":["The population height mean and standard deviation stay fixed, but the three random samples have different sample means and sample standard deviations. Each sample contains a different selection of individuals.","A sample with more tall individuals can have a mean above the population mean; another with more short individuals can have a mean below it. The variation comes from who was selected, even when the population has not changed.","Thus \\(\\bar{x}\\) estimates \\(\\mu\\), and \\(s\\) estimates \\(\\sigma\\). The next step is to describe how much those estimates fluctuate across repeated samples."]},"2:16":{"title":"A distribution made from sample means","paragraphs":["Imagine repeatedly selecting ten individuals and calculating their average height. Each completed sample contributes one mean to this new distribution, rather than ten individual heights.","Some samples produce a slightly low average and others a slightly high average. Plotting those averages reveals how much a mean from a sample of this size tends to vary.","Keep the unit of observation clear: each point now stands for an entire sample's average. The width of this distribution describes uncertainty in estimating the population mean."]},"2:17":{"title":"Individual heights versus averages of ten heights","paragraphs":["The height distribution describes variation from one individual to another. The distribution of averages of ten individuals is narrower because unusually high and low measurements partly balance during averaging.","Both distributions remain centered near the same population height. Averaging changes how much the result varies, rather than intentionally shifting its expected center.","This is why an average from several independent measurements is usually more stable than one measurement. The narrower curve belongs to sample means, so its standard deviation has a different meaning from the standard deviation of individual heights."]},"2:18":{"title":"The central limit theorem and standard error","paragraphs":["The sample mean is itself a random variable: it changes whenever the selected sample changes. Its expected center is the population mean.","Its spread is the population standard deviation divided by the square root of the sample size. This spread is called the standard error of the mean, because it describes variation among sample means.","The central limit theorem explains why averages from sufficiently large independent samples become approximately Normal even when individual measurements are not. The course gives a sample-size guideline of 30; increasing sample size also makes those averages less scattered."]},"2:19":{"title":"Estimating standard error from one sample","paragraphs":["Usually, only one sample is available, rather than a whole distribution of repeated samples. Calculate its mean and standard deviation first, using the observations actually collected.","Replace the unknown population standard deviation in the standard-error expression with the sample standard deviation. Dividing \\(s\\) by the square root of \\(n\\) estimates the spread that repeated sample means would have.","The separate expression for uncertainty in the sample variance concerns a different estimate. Keep the target attached to each standard error: uncertainty in a mean and uncertainty in a variance are not interchangeable."]},"2:20":{"title":"Measurements, counts, and categories","paragraphs":["Continuous numerical data can vary along a measurement scale, such as blood pressure or height. Discrete numerical data count separate outcomes, such as the number of tender joints or asthma episodes.","Categorical data label groups. Ordinal categories have a meaningful order, such as increasing cancer stage or pain severity; nominal categories have no natural ranking, such as causes of death.","Choose the description from what the variable means, rather than whether its labels happen to be numbers. A numbered disease stage remains an ordered category, whereas a count records how many events occurred."]},"2:21":{"title":"Classifying the student survey variables","paragraphs":["The survey mixes measurements and group labels. Finger length, height, and heart rate record numerical quantities, while eye color, handedness, and pet preference identify categories.","Class level has an order, but the labels represent stages of education. That makes its interpretation different from measuring a distance or counting heartbeats during a fixed interval.","Think about one student's response in each column: what was actually observed, and what would a difference between two responses mean? That question determines which summaries and comparisons make sense."]},"2:22":{"title":"The class heart-rate histogram","paragraphs":["This histogram describes the heart rates of the entire surveyed class, treated as the population for this example. The bars show how many students fall into each heart-rate range.","The population mean is about 72 beats per minute, and the standard deviation describes variation among individual students. The interquartile range instead describes the width occupied by the middle half of their heart rates.","Read shape and summary together. The mean locates the center, while the histogram reveals how the observations are arranged around it, including unusually low or high measurements."]},"2:23":{"title":"The class heart-rate box plot","paragraphs":["The median heart rate is 70.5 beats per minute. The box extends from 61.5 to 80, so the central half of the class falls within that range.","The labeled minimum and maximum show how far the full set extends. Those extreme observations are different from the quartiles: only one observation may determine an extreme, whereas the box summarizes half the data.","A box plot compresses the class into a few positions on one scale. Use it to locate the center and spread without assuming that all heart rates inside the box occur equally often."]},"2:24":{"title":"The class heart-rate violin plot","paragraphs":["The violin presents the same class heart rates with their distribution shape visible. Its outline shows where values cluster, while the central marks retain the median and quartiles.","The first and third quartiles still bound the middle half of the class. Changing from a box plot to a violin plot does not change the observations or their summary values.","Compare the width of the outline at different heart rates. A broad region means more students have values near that part of the scale, adding detail that a box alone cannot show."]},"2:25":{"title":"Matching a plot to the kind of data","paragraphs":["The height and finger-length violin plots summarize numerical measurements. Their widths show concentration along a measurement scale, and their central marks locate typical values.","The eye-color display uses separate category bars. Those bars count membership in named groups rather than dividing a continuous measurement scale into neighboring intervals.","The height-versus-finger-length scatter plot uses one point per student and places two measurements on two axes. This arrangement reveals relationships between measurements, rather than merely summarizing either measurement by itself."]},"2:26":{"title":"Dog-preferring and cat-preferring students","paragraphs":["The two groups differ in size, so raw histogram counts need to be read with their group totals in mind. The dog-preferring group also has a wider heart-rate spread in these class data.","The group means are relatively close, while their standard deviations describe substantial differences among individuals within each group. A difference between two sample averages must therefore be considered alongside that background variation.","The paired histograms and violins show both center and shape. They set up the later question of whether a smaller sample from each group provides convincing evidence of a population difference."]},"2:27":{"title":"Turning a sample comparison into a statistical question","paragraphs":["Now select five students from each pet-preference group and compare their average heart rates. The two averages will usually differ somewhat even if the underlying population means are equal.","A statistical test asks whether the observed difference is large relative to the sampling variation expected when there is no population difference. It connects what happened in the samples to a statement about the populations.","The target is the population mean heart rate in each group. Use the sample means, their variability, and the sample sizes to determine how surprising the observed separation is under an equal-means assumption."]},"3:1":{"title":"Probability gives a language for uncertain outcomes","paragraphs":["A random variable records an outcome that is not known in advance. Some outcomes take separate values, such as a count of heads; others vary continuously, such as a measured height.","For discrete outcomes, identify which values can occur and how likely each value is. A probability function lists those individual chances, while a distribution function adds chances up to a chosen cutoff.","Expected value describes the probability-weighted center. Independence explains when one event leaves another event's chance unchanged, which is the key to understanding the gambler's fallacy."]},"3:2":{"title":"Organizing data before plotting","paragraphs":["Indexing selects particular entries from a collection of values. A data frame groups related variables so that measurements from the same observation remain together while the data are inspected or rearranged.","Input and output move data into or out of R. Plotting then changes the form of the display: line charts, histograms, boxes, and violins emphasize different features of the same measurements.","Saving a plot preserves the display for later use. The important connection is between the structure of the data and the question the plot answers, such as change across positions or the distribution of individual values."]},"3:3":{"title":"A known population before probability","paragraphs":["The Martian heights provide a population whose center and spread can be calculated directly. Every height contributes to the population mean, and every squared distance from that mean contributes to the variance.","The mean is 40 cm and the standard deviation is 5 cm. These describe the full population, rather than estimates from a small selected sample.","Before an individual is randomly selected, the height of that individual is uncertain. The same population picture can then describe which heights are more common and which are less common, linking descriptive statistics to probability."]},"3:4":{"title":"Why probability was developed","paragraphs":["Questions about gambling helped motivate a mathematical treatment of uncertain outcomes. Pascal and Fermat considered such problems, followed by contributions from Bayes and Laplace.","The possible outcomes may be known even when the particular outcome remains uncertain. Probability makes that uncertainty describable and allows different uncertain events to be compared.","The quotation emphasizes uncertainty as an ordinary feature of life. In this course, the practical use is biomedical measurement: a population's pattern supports predictions about outcomes that are not yet observed."]},"3:5":{"title":"Process, variable, experiment, and sample space","paragraphs":["A random process has known possible results but an uncertain actual result. An experiment is the procedure that produces an outcome or collects a measurement from that process.","A random variable records the outcome of interest. Its value is unknown before the experiment but becomes a particular observed value afterward.","The sample space contains all possible experimental outcomes. Keep the procedure, the recorded quantity, and the set of possibilities separate: they describe different parts of the same probability problem."]},"3:6":{"title":"Three ways to define a random outcome","paragraphs":["For one coin toss, the recorded outcome can be heads or tails. The experiment is the toss, and the uncertainty concerns which face appears.","For a randomly selected student, the recorded outcome can instead be heart rate. The selection and measurement produce a numerical observation whose value is not known beforehand.","For three coin tosses, the total number of heads can be recorded instead of the complete sequence. Different sequences can give the same count, so defining the random variable determines how outcomes are grouped."]},"3:7":{"title":"Counting outcomes in an event","paragraphs":["An event collects the outcomes that satisfy a condition. For an equally likely set of elementary outcomes, count how many belong to the event and compare that count with the total number possible.","The numerator counts favorable outcomes; the denominator counts the full sample space. This gives a proportion between zero and one.","The equal-likelihood condition matters when using simple counts. A loaded die, for example, requires the actual probabilities of its outcomes rather than treating each face as equally common."]},"3:8":{"title":"Coding a coin toss as a discrete variable","paragraphs":["The coin has two outcomes, heads and tails. Assigning them the numerical codes 1 and 2 allows their probabilities to be written using a random variable \\(X\\).","For a fair coin, both codes have probability one half. The number is a label for the outcome; changing the label does not change the physical coin toss.","Uppercase \\(X\\) names the variable before it is observed, while lowercase \\(x\\) refers to one possible value. Asking for the probability that the variable equals a particular value selects that outcome."]},"3:9":{"title":"A fair die and six possible values","paragraphs":["Here the random variable is the number of dots on the upward face. Its possible values are the integers from one through six.","A fair die assigns the same probability to each face. The six probabilities together account for every possible result, so they sum to one.","If a question selects several faces, add their probabilities. For example, an event described by a property of the dots may contain more than one of the six elementary outcomes."]},"3:10":{"title":"Grouping die outcomes into odd and even","paragraphs":["The experiment still rolls a die, but the recorded variable now indicates odd or even. Code odd as zero and even as one.","Three die faces produce each code, so each coded result has probability one half. The variable has two possible values even though the underlying die has six faces.","This shows why defining the recorded quantity comes first. A question about the exact face uses six outcomes, whereas a question about parity combines those faces into two groups."]},"3:11":{"title":"Counting heads in three tosses","paragraphs":["Three tosses produce eight equally likely sequences. The random variable records only the number of heads, so several sequences can produce the same value.","There is one sequence with no heads and one with three heads. There are three sequences with one head and three with two heads.","Thus the four possible counts are not equally likely. Count how many sequences lead to each value before assigning its probability; the count values themselves do not reveal their likelihoods."]},"3:12":{"title":"NAT2 metabolic phenotypes","paragraphs":["NAT2 is an enzyme involved in metabolizing drugs. The example groups infants into slow, intermediate, and fast metabolic phenotypes, with a separate probability assigned to each category.","The codes 1, 2, and 3 label those phenotypes. Intermediate metabolism is the most common of the three in the displayed distribution, but every category has a nonzero chance.","A randomly selected infant contributes one category. The probabilities describe the population pattern of category membership, rather than three possible amounts of enzyme measured on an evenly spaced scale."]},"3:13":{"title":"A run of heads before any tosses occur","paragraphs":["To get a specified run of heads, every toss in the run must be heads. Independence allows the one-half probabilities to be multiplied across the required tosses.","As the required run gets longer, the probability gets smaller because more conditions must all be satisfied. That statement concerns the entire sequence before its results are known.","Once three heads have already occurred, the next toss is a different question. The earlier tosses are established facts; only the next toss remains uncertain."]},"3:14":{"title":"Why the next toss is still one half","paragraphs":["After three heads, a fair coin still has a one-half chance of heads on its next toss. The coin has not changed its behavior in response to the previous results.","The small probability of four heads in a row includes uncertainty about all four tosses before the sequence begins. It is not the probability of the fourth head after the first three are already known.","The gambler's fallacy confuses these questions, sometimes treating tails as due after a run of heads. Independence means past results do not make the next fair toss compensate for them."]},"3:15":{"title":"When an earlier event changes the next chance","paragraphs":["Repeated coin tosses leave the next toss's possibilities unchanged. Drawing a card and replacing it before reshuffling similarly restores the original deck.","Without replacement, the first card is removed. That changes both the number of cards left and, depending on the first result, the number of desired cards remaining.","Dependence therefore comes from a changed situation for the second event. Before multiplying probabilities, ask whether the first action alters what can happen next or how likely it is."]},"3:16":{"title":"Multiplying independent and conditional probabilities","paragraphs":["For a die roll of six followed by an even result, the second roll starts fresh. Multiply the chance of six by the chance of an even result.","For two kings drawn without replacement, the second draw is from a deck with one fewer card and one fewer king. Its chance must be calculated after the first king has been removed.","The vertical bar in a conditional probability means given that the earlier event occurred. Use that updated probability for the second step whenever the first event changes the remaining possibilities."]},"3:17":{"title":"Writing the coin's probability function","paragraphs":["A probability function pairs each possible value with its chance. For the coded fair coin, both 1 and 2 receive probability one half.","Values outside that allowed set receive probability zero. The function therefore describes the complete variable, including which values cannot occur.","The graph places probability at separate positions because the variable is discrete. Each height is a probability for one exact value, unlike a continuous density where probability comes from area across an interval."]},"3:18":{"title":"A uniform discrete probability function","paragraphs":["The fair die puts equal probability at each integer from one to six. Uniform here means that every allowed value has the same probability.","The probability function is zero at other values. There is no chance of rolling a value between faces, such as three and a half.","To find the chance of a set of allowed faces, add the corresponding equal masses. The graph's separate spikes make the distinction between individual values and a continuous measurement scale visible."]},"3:19":{"title":"A loaded die changes the weights","paragraphs":["This die favors the face with one dot. Its probability is 0.20, while each of the other five faces has probability 0.16.","The allowed values have not changed, but their weights have. Adding one larger mass and five smaller masses still gives a total probability of one.","A loaded die therefore cannot be analyzed by counting desired faces alone. Use the probability attached to each selected face and add those probabilities to calculate an event's chance."]},"3:20":{"title":"From eight sequences to four probability masses","paragraphs":["List the eight possible three-toss sequences, then group them by their head counts. The three ways to obtain two heads belong to one value of the random variable.","That grouping creates probabilities of one eighth at the two extremes and three eighths at each middle count. Middle counts are more common because more sequences lead to them.","The probability function describes counts rather than sequence identities. Once the grouping is done, a question about at least two heads can be answered by adding the masses for two and three."]},"3:21":{"title":"A probability function for metabolic categories","paragraphs":["The slow, intermediate, and fast NAT2 categories form a discrete set. Their displayed probabilities describe how a randomly selected infant is distributed among those three outcomes.","Intermediate metabolism carries the largest probability mass. The other two categories remain separate outcomes, with different probabilities rather than equal shares by default.","These category probabilities support questions such as the chance of selecting a slow or intermediate metabolizer. Add the relevant category masses while keeping the biological meaning of the numerical labels attached."]},"3:22":{"title":"The general discrete probability picture","paragraphs":["A discrete variable can have several allowed values, each with its own probability. The values determine where the graph has masses, and the probabilities determine their heights.","The masses need not be evenly spaced or equal in size. A value with a taller probability mass occurs more often over repeated experiments than a value with a shorter one.","Outside the allowed set, probability is zero. To answer a probability question, identify the allowed values satisfying its condition and add exactly their masses."]},"3:23":{"title":"Checking that a probability function is valid","paragraphs":["Every individual probability must be nonnegative. A negative mass would assign a negative chance to an outcome, which has no probability interpretation.","The probabilities across all possible values must also sum to one. That total accounts for the certainty that some allowed outcome occurs.","Check both requirements before calculating an expectation or a cumulative probability. A set of numbers can look like a probability table while failing either the sign requirement or the total-probability requirement."]},"3:24":{"title":"A cumulative function adds probability up to a cutoff","paragraphs":["The distribution function reports the chance that the variable is at or below a chosen value. For the coded coin, it is zero before the first allowed value, then one half, then one.","The cumulative graph jumps where a probability mass is added. Between allowed values, nothing new enters the total, so the graph stays flat.","For the loaded die, begin with the larger mass at one and add each subsequent mass as the cutoff crosses another face value. The cumulative function never decreases because probabilities are being accumulated."]},"3:25":{"title":"Sketching the function with probabilities proportional to value","paragraphs":["The allowed values are 1, 2, and 3, with probability proportional to the value. The largest value therefore has the largest mass, and the three masses together sum to one.","For the probability-function sketch, place a separate mass at each allowed value. For the cumulative sketch, start at zero and add those masses from left to right.","The cumulative graph jumps by the size of the mass being crossed. Include the mass at the cutoff itself, because the distribution function includes values equal to its argument."]},"3:26":{"title":"Expected value is a weighted average","paragraphs":["For the coded coin, average the possible values using their probabilities as weights. The result is 1.5, even though a single toss never produces that code.","Expectation describes the center across repeated outcomes, not a result that must itself be possible. A value contributes more when it is larger or more probable.","The sum over possible values weights by probability; the population average sums over individuals. They describe the same center when the probabilities represent how frequently those values occur in the population."]},"3:27":{"title":"Taking the expectation after changing the outcome","paragraphs":["A function of a random variable changes each possible value before averaging. Squaring the variable means square each allowed value first, then apply its original probability weight.","Squared distance from the mean is another function. For each possible outcome, calculate how far it is from the mean and square that distance.","Keep the order of operations clear: transform each outcome, then average the transformed values. Squaring an already calculated mean usually gives a different result from averaging the squared outcomes."]},"3:28":{"title":"Variance as an expected squared distance","paragraphs":["The coded coin has possible values one and two, centered at 1.5. Each outcome is half a unit from that center.","Squaring those distances makes both contributions positive. Their probability-weighted average is the variance, which measures spread around the expected value.","Expected squared value and variance are related but different quantities. The first uses distance from zero, while variance uses distance from the mean; keeping that reference point visible prevents the two calculations from becoming confused."]},"3:29":{"title":"Mean and variance for the three-value example","paragraphs":["Use the probabilities attached to 1, 2, and 3 as weights when calculating the mean. The larger values receive more weight in this distribution, so the center shifts toward them.","For variance, calculate each value's distance from that weighted mean, square it, and multiply by the same probability. Add the resulting weighted squared distances.","The same probability table supplies both calculations, but the quantity being averaged changes. The mean averages values; the variance averages their squared distances from the mean."]},"3:30":{"title":"Roulette earnings include losing outcomes","paragraphs":["A single-number roulette bet has a small chance of a payout and a much larger chance of losing the stake. The earnings variable records net gain or loss from one play.","The displayed distribution assigns a loss of one unit to the losing outcomes and a gain of 35 units to the winning outcome. These are earnings, not the numbers printed on the wheel.","A probability model must include both sides of that result. The large possible win is only one part of the calculation; the many losing outcomes carry most of the probability weight."]},"3:31":{"title":"Expected roulette earnings","paragraphs":["Multiply each net earnings outcome by its probability and add the contributions. The frequent one-unit losses pull the weighted average downward, while the rare 35-unit gain pulls it upward.","The resulting expectation is negative because the winning payout does not fully offset the probability-weighted losses. That describes average earnings over repeated plays rather than the outcome of one particular spin.","A large possible gain can therefore coexist with a negative expectation. Read both the size of an outcome and how often it occurs before judging the center of an earnings distribution."]},"4:1":{"title":"From separate outcomes to continuous measurements","paragraphs":["A continuous random variable can take values throughout a range. A density curve describes its probabilities, rather than assigning a separate probability to each possible measurement.","The distribution function accumulates area under that curve up to a cutoff. It turns the shape of a density into the probability of being below a particular measurement.","Expected value then gives a probability-weighted center. The central change from discrete variables is that areas and integrals replace sums of separate probability masses."]},"4:2":{"title":"Different summaries answer different questions","paragraphs":["Mean, median, trimmed mean, and mode all describe center, but they use the data differently. The median uses ordered position, while a trimmed mean removes selected extremes before averaging.","Range, interquartile range, variance, and standard deviation describe different aspects of spread. Skewness and kurtosis add information about asymmetry and tails.","The R summary functions collect these descriptions for a data set or for separate groups. Read the name of the summary alongside its value so that a measure of spread is not mistaken for a measure of center."]},"4:3":{"title":"Extending the discrete framework","paragraphs":["For a discrete variable, the probability function attaches a mass to each allowed value. Those masses are nonnegative and sum to one.","An expectation multiplies each transformed value by its probability and adds the contributions. Choosing the transformation to be the value itself gives the mean; choosing squared distance from the mean gives variance.","The continuous framework keeps those same meanings. What changes is how probability is distributed: it occupies intervals under a curve rather than separate points on the number line."]},"4:4":{"title":"Accumulating the coded coin's probabilities","paragraphs":["The probability graph for the coded coin has masses at one and two. The cumulative function adds every mass at or below the cutoff.","Before one, the cumulative probability is zero. After including one it is one half, and after including two it is one; the graph stays flat between those changes.","The loaded-die exercise follows the same rule with more jumps and unequal masses. The jump at a particular value equals that value's probability."]},"4:5":{"title":"Continuous biomedical outcomes","paragraphs":["Body temperature and forced expiratory volume are measured along a continuous scale. The viral-load example likewise treats the measured concentration as a variable over a range.","For such measurements, the useful probability question concerns an interval: how likely is the result to fall between two values? Narrower intervals generally capture less probability than wider intervals in the same region.","The possible range describes where measurements can occur. A probability density supplies the additional information about which parts of that range are more common."]},"4:6":{"title":"Probability is area under the density","paragraphs":["The heart-rate density places measurement values along the horizontal axis. The chance of a heart rate between 75 and 100 beats per minute is the area under the curve between those boundaries.","A density must stay nonnegative, and its entire area must equal one. Its height alone is not an interval probability; both the shape and the width of the selected region matter.","An integral adds the thin strips of area across the interval. Think of it as collecting all the probability between the two cutoffs, rather than substituting one cutoff into the density."]},"4:7":{"title":"A uniform density has constant height","paragraphs":["Between its lower and upper bounds, a uniform density is flat. Equal-length intervals inside those bounds therefore have equal probability.","The rectangle's full area must be one, so its height is one divided by the total interval width. A wider range requires a shorter rectangle to keep that same total area.","The standard Uniform distribution uses bounds zero and one. For any uniform example, an interval's probability is its length divided by the full allowed length."]},"4:8":{"title":"Center and width of a Normal density","paragraphs":["The Normal density is bell shaped and symmetric around its mean. The mean moves the center along the measurement axis.","The standard deviation stretches or compresses the curve. Increasing it spreads the same total probability over a wider range, making the central peak lower.","In the notation shown, the second Normal parameter is variance, \\(\\sigma^2\\). When you need a standard deviation for standardizing a measurement, take the square root of that variance."]},"4:9":{"title":"The standard Normal reference curve","paragraphs":["The standard Normal variable is named \\(Z\\). Its mean is zero and its standard deviation is one, providing a common scale for Normal probability calculations.","A position on this axis describes distance from the center in standard-deviation units. Negative values lie below the mean and positive values lie above it.","The curve is still a density, so probabilities are areas. A table of cumulative areas provides probabilities for this reference curve, which then translate to other Normal measurements."]},"4:10":{"title":"Building a continuous cumulative distribution","paragraphs":["The cumulative distribution function collects density area from the far left up to a chosen value. Moving the cutoff rightward includes more of the curve.","The accumulated probability begins at zero and approaches one once the full density is included. It cannot decrease because moving right never removes area that was already counted.","Lowercase \\(f\\) names the density, while uppercase \\(F\\) names accumulated probability. The integration variable inside the expression is a placeholder for the values being added, not another measured outcome."]},"4:11":{"title":"Turning flat and triangular densities into cumulative curves","paragraphs":["For the flat density, equal movements along the measurement axis add equal amounts of area. Its cumulative function therefore rises at a constant rate within the allowed range.","For the triangular density, area initially accumulates slowly, then more quickly as the density gets taller. After the peak, each additional interval contributes less area again.","Both cumulative curves start at zero and finish at one. Their different slopes reflect the different density heights: a steeper cumulative curve means more probability is being collected in that region."]},"4:12":{"title":"The uniform cumulative function","paragraphs":["Before the lower bound of a Uniform distribution, no density area has been included. The cumulative probability is therefore zero.","Inside the allowed interval, the accumulated rectangle grows in direct proportion to how far the cutoff has moved from the lower bound. The cumulative graph is a straight rising line there.","At the upper bound, all the density has been included and the cumulative probability reaches one. Beyond that point it stays one because there is no additional probability outside the allowed interval."]},"4:13":{"title":"A triangular density needs separate pieces","paragraphs":["This density rises from zero to its peak over the first part of the range, then falls back toward zero over the second part. Its defining expression changes where the two sides meet.","To find cumulative probability on the first side, integrate the rising part only. On the second side, include the entire first portion before adding the area under the falling portion.","The cumulative function must join smoothly in value at the meeting point. It continues increasing even when the density falls, because a smaller positive density still contributes additional area."]},"4:14":{"title":"Left tails, right tails, and intervals","paragraphs":["The cumulative function gives the probability at or below a cutoff. A right-tail probability is the part left over after subtracting that accumulated probability from one.","For a probability between two cutoffs, subtract the cumulative probability at the lower cutoff from the cumulative probability at the upper cutoff. The subtraction removes the area lying too far to the left.","The density picture shows the area selected, and the cumulative picture shows how much has accumulated at each boundary. They are two ways of describing the same probability."]},"4:15":{"title":"Why a Uniform mean is the midpoint","paragraphs":["Expectation weights measurement values by their density across the allowed interval. A Uniform density gives equal weight to equal-length regions.","Values equally far to the left and right of the midpoint balance each other. The expected value therefore sits halfway between the lower and upper bounds.","The integral in the example expresses that balance mathematically: multiply each position by the constant density and accumulate across the range. The result agrees with the midpoint visible from the symmetric rectangle."]},"4:16":{"title":"Applying a linear change before averaging","paragraphs":["A new variable can be defined by adding a constant and multiplying the original measurement by a constant. The expression \\(Y=a+bX\\) describes that shift and rescaling.","To find the expected new value directly, apply the transformation to every possible original value and weight it by the original density. The density still determines the probability weights of those original values.","The shift adds the same amount to every outcome, while the multiplier scales every outcome. These simple effects lead to a useful shortcut for the new mean."]},"4:17":{"title":"Separating the shift from the rescaling","paragraphs":["The expectation integral can be split into the constant part and the part involving the original variable. Constants can be moved outside their integrals.","The density integrates to one, so the constant part contributes exactly \\(a\\). The remaining integral is the original expected value multiplied by \\(b\\).","Thus the mean changes in the same linear way as an individual measurement. Adding a constant shifts the mean, and multiplying the measurements scales the mean; there is no need to recompute the entire distribution."]},"4:18":{"title":"Variance for a Uniform measurement from 20 to 40","paragraphs":["The uniform range is centered at 30. Variance describes how far values lie from that center by averaging squared distances across the full interval.","The density is constant, so every equal-width part of the interval receives the same probability weight. Values near 20 or 40 contribute larger squared distances than values near 30.","The integration limits come from the allowed range. The result is a variance in squared measurement units; taking its square root would return the spread to the original units."]},"4:19":{"title":"Two useful expectation identities","paragraphs":["Expanding the squared distance from the mean produces terms involving the squared variable, the variable, and constants. Taking expectations of those terms gives a shortcut for variance using a second moment and the mean.","For a linear transformation, adding a constant changes every value and its mean by the same amount. Their differences therefore remain unchanged.","Multiplying by \\(b\\) multiplies every distance from the mean by \\(b\\). Squaring those distances makes variance scale by \\(b^2\\), which explains why variance and standard deviation transform differently."]},"4:20":{"title":"A density for blood-sample analysis time","paragraphs":["The analysis time ranges from zero to two hours, and its density rises with time. Longer times receive more probability per unit of time than shorter times within that range.","First accumulate the density to build the cumulative function. Then the chance of finishing within one hour is a left-tail probability, while the chance of taking longer than one and a half hours is a right-tail probability.","Expected time weights time by its density. The median instead finds the time where half the density area has accumulated, so these two central summaries come from different questions."]},"4:21":{"title":"Organizing the five analysis-time questions","paragraphs":["Use the cumulative function as the starting point for the probability parts. Evaluating it at one hour gives the chance of finishing by then; subtracting its value at one and a half hours from one gives the remaining tail.","The expected-time calculation averages possible times with density weights. It requires a factor for the time itself as well as the density.","For the median, set cumulative probability to one half and solve for the time. Check that every answer belongs to the zero-to-two-hour range and distinguish a time-valued answer from a probability-valued answer."]},"5:1":{"title":"Using one Normal reference for many measurements","paragraphs":["The continuous-variable summary connects density, accumulated probability, and expected value. These describe a distribution's shape, chances, and center.","The standard Normal variable provides a common reference curve. Once a measurement is converted into standard-deviation units, the same probability table can be used for many different Normal populations.","The reverse process identifies a cutoff for a requested probability. Finally, Normal quantile plots compare observed data with the pattern expected from a Normal population."]},"5:2":{"title":"Descriptive summaries before inference","paragraphs":["The discussion outline returns to methods for describing a sample. Center can be expressed with a mean, median, trimmed mean, or mode, depending on the feature being summarized.","Spread includes range, interquartile range, variance, and standard deviation. Shape adds another layer through skewness and kurtosis.","The listed R functions produce these summaries for a full data set or separate groups. Keep the conceptual question attached to each output: where the data sit, how widely they vary, or what shape their distribution has."]},"5:3":{"title":"Density area and accumulated probability","paragraphs":["The shaded region between two measurement boundaries is an interval probability. It is obtained by accumulating the density across that interval.","The cumulative function collects all area to the left of a cutoff. Its value at the upper boundary includes the desired interval plus everything farther left.","Subtracting the cumulative value at the lower boundary removes that extra left-hand area. This is the link between an integral over an interval and a difference of two cumulative probabilities."]},"5:4":{"title":"Expected values of continuous outcomes","paragraphs":["The mean of a continuous variable is a density-weighted average of its possible values. Values contribute according to both their size and how much probability density surrounds them.","For a transformed variable, apply the transformation to each original value before weighting and accumulating. This covers squared values and squared distances as well as linear changes.","The density remains the weighting rule for the original variable. Changing the quantity being averaged changes the factor multiplied by that density, rather than making every possible value equally likely."]},"5:5":{"title":"Choosing the right cumulative subtraction","paragraphs":["A cumulative probability gives the area to the left. For a right tail, remove that left-hand area from the total probability of one.","For a bounded interval, compare the accumulated probability at its two ends. The larger cumulative value contains the smaller one plus the requested interval.","The two graphs connect these operations: the density marks the selected region, while the cumulative curve records how much probability has accumulated at each cutoff. Start with the region before choosing the subtraction."]},"5:6":{"title":"The standard Normal density and its cumulative function","paragraphs":["The standard Normal density is centered at zero with standard deviation one. Its height is largest near the center and decreases toward both tails.","Its cumulative function rises from zero to one, passing through one half at zero. The curve rises most quickly where the density is largest.","The table records cumulative probabilities, not density heights. When you read a table entry for a standard Normal value, you are finding the area to its left."]},"5:7":{"title":"Normal examples: left, right, and between","paragraphs":["A cutoff of 1.5 uses the table's left-tail value directly. A request above 0.5 instead uses one minus the left-tail value at 0.5.","For an interval, subtract the lower cutoff's cumulative value from the upper cutoff's value. This works even when the interval crosses zero, because both table values use the same left-hand starting point.","Symmetry lets a negative tail be related to its positive counterpart. The example below minus two equals the upper tail above plus two; the reflected areas match even though their positions differ."]},"5:8":{"title":"Translating each probability statement into an area","paragraphs":["Read each inequality before opening the table. A single upper boundary asks for a left area; a single lower boundary asks for a right area; two boundaries ask for their difference.","The intervals centered at zero collect matching pieces on both sides of the mean. Wider symmetric intervals include more of the bell curve.","The R function pnorm returns cumulative probability at its input. It can check a table-based answer, but a right tail or interval still requires the same complement or subtraction used by hand."]},"5:9":{"title":"Quantiles reverse the probability question","paragraphs":["A quantile question gives an area and asks where to place the cutoff. Search for the requested cumulative probability in the table, then read the associated standard Normal value.","If the probability is a right tail, convert it into the area to the left first. For a central symmetric interval, divide the leftover probability between the two tails before locating the positive boundary.","The R function qnorm performs this reverse lookup. Keep its direction separate from pnorm: one starts with a probability and returns a cutoff, while the other starts with a cutoff and returns a probability."]},"5:10":{"title":"Standardizing a Normal measurement","paragraphs":["Subtracting the mean moves the center to zero. Dividing by the standard deviation measures that distance in units of the population's spread.","The resulting standard Normal value says how many standard deviations the observation lies above or below the mean. The original units cancel in the conversion.","To reverse the conversion, multiply the standard Normal value by the original standard deviation and add the original mean. This takes a table cutoff back to the measurement scale of the actual problem."]},"5:11":{"title":"Standardizing preserves the selected probability","paragraphs":["A cutoff on the original Normal scale and its standardized cutoff describe the same position relative to the distribution. Rescaling changes the axis, not the probability in the selected region.","A left-tail event therefore becomes a standard Normal left-tail event. A right tail still requires a complement after the boundary is standardized.","For an interval, standardize both boundaries using the same mean and standard deviation. Subtract the two cumulative standard Normal values to recover the original interval's probability."]},"5:12":{"title":"Arterial oxygen pressure as a Normal calculation","paragraphs":["Arterial oxygen pressure has a mean of 90 mmHg and a standard deviation of 7 mmHg in this example. The notation squares the standard deviation in the variance position.","To find the chance below 110 mmHg, express 110 as a distance from 90 measured in units of 7. Use the cumulative table at that standardized cutoff.","For the range from 80 to 110, standardize both ends. The upper cumulative probability includes values below 80 as well, so subtract the lower cumulative probability to leave only the requested interval."]},"5:13":{"title":"Why the familiar Normal percentages recur","paragraphs":["A boundary one standard deviation above the mean always standardizes to plus one. A boundary one below always standardizes to minus one, whatever the original units are.","The same reasoning turns two-standard-deviation and three-standard-deviation intervals into fixed intervals on the standard Normal scale. Their probabilities are therefore shared by every Normal distribution.","This explains the familiar central proportions as areas, rather than isolated facts to memorize. The interval becomes wider in standard-deviation units, so it captures progressively more of the distribution."]},"5:14":{"title":"Impurity averaged across fifty batches","paragraphs":["The question concerns the mean impurity across fifty independent batches. Its center remains 4 grams, but the average varies less than a single batch's impurity.","Use the standard error of the mean, rather than the individual-batch standard deviation, when standardizing the two boundaries. Averaging makes the requested interval sit at different standardized distances than it would for one batch.","Both 3.5 and 3.8 grams lie below the population mean. Find their two cumulative probabilities and subtract in upper-minus-lower order to obtain the chance that the sample mean falls between them."]},"5:15":{"title":"Comparing observed data with a Normal pattern","paragraphs":["The earlier check counted observations within selected distances of the sample mean. A Normal population predicts characteristic proportions in those regions.","Now the comparison is extended to the full distribution rather than a few chosen bands. The Normal density provides a reference for where observations at different cumulative positions should lie.","If the data resemble that reference throughout their range, a Normal model is plausible. The next plots turn this comparison into a visual relationship between observed and theoretical quantiles."]},"5:16":{"title":"Why a Normal quantile plot should look straight","paragraphs":["A quantile identifies a position within an ordered distribution. A Normal quantile plot pairs each ordered sample value with the standard Normal value expected at its cumulative position.","If the population is Normal, the sample values are approximately a shifted and rescaled version of the theoretical values. That relationship produces an approximately straight line.","Random sampling introduces some departures even for Normal data. Look for the overall pattern and systematic curvature rather than expecting every point to lie exactly on the reference line."]},"5:17":{"title":"Reading the plotted Normality examples","paragraphs":["The ten-observation example is plotted against theoretical Normal positions. Its points roughly follow a straight line, which is the pattern expected when the data are compatible with a Normal population.","The contrasting examples show systematic departures from straightness. Curvature or unusual behavior at the ends indicates that parts of the observed distribution differ from the Normal reference.","The functions shown create the point plot and add a reference line. Interpret the shape of the resulting graph: the purpose is to compare the distributional pattern, rather than to memorize the command sequence."]},"6:1":{"title":"Comparing group means with ANOVA","paragraphs":["The question is whether different groups have the same population mean. Observed sample means alone cannot settle that question because random sampling produces some differences even without a treatment effect.","ANOVA compares two estimates of population variance. One comes from variation among individuals within groups; the other comes from the separation of group means.","Their ratio is the F statistic. The cell-phone example applies this idea to sperm motility, connecting a biological group comparison to a calculation of between-group separation relative to background variation."]},"6:2":{"title":"From probability areas to a group-comparison test","paragraphs":["Probability calculations turn cutoffs into areas and areas into cutoffs. The listed Normal functions support those two directions, as in the preceding hand calculations.","ANOVA adds a different reference distribution for a statistic built from group data. Its F value compares variation between groups with variation within them.","The hospital-stay and antidepressant examples identify an outcome measurement and a grouping variable. The task is to decide whether the observed outcome differences are unusually large under an equal-population-means assumption."]},"6:3":{"title":"The diet and cardiac-output experiment","paragraphs":["Four groups receive four different diets: a normal diet, pasta, steak, or fruit and nuts. Each group contains seven people, and cardiac output is measured after one month.","The normal-diet group supplies a control condition. The outcome is cardiac output, so each person contributes a numerical measurement rather than simply a diet label.","The null hypothesis says all four diets have the same population mean cardiac output. Any observed sample differences must then be explained by variation among people and by which people were selected."]},"6:4":{"title":"Seeing the full population when diet has no effect","paragraphs":["The dots show the cardiac-output measurements for the full hypothetical population. The different diet labels occupy the same overall distribution rather than distinct treatment-shifted populations.","The Normal notation describes a shared center and shared variance. If diet has no effect, group membership does not move the population mean for cardiac output.","This full-population picture provides the reference situation. In practice, only a few sampled people from each group are observed, so their sample means can separate even when this underlying equal-means picture is true."]},"6:5":{"title":"Sample mean differences versus individual variation","paragraphs":["Each group has a sample mean and a spread of individual cardiac-output values. The means are not identical, but that alone does not establish that the diets caused different population outcomes.","Ask how large the separation of the means is compared with the spread inside the groups. Widely scattered individual values can produce visibly different sample means just by sampling.","ANOVA formalizes this comparison. The numerator will reflect separation among group means, while the denominator will reflect the ordinary variation observed within the groups."]},"6:6":{"title":"Group measurements and the spread of their means","paragraphs":["The separate group rows show individual measurements around each group's center. The lower summary collects the group centers and shows how widely those centers vary.","There are therefore two levels in the picture: variation among people and variation among sample means. A group mean summarizes several people, so its expected variability is smaller than individual variability.","The question asks whether the displayed spread among means is consistent with the spread within the groups. This is the visual comparison that the two variance estimates will express numerically."]},"6:7":{"title":"Reading the second group-variation illustration","paragraphs":["Follow the individual points in each group, then compare their centers. The bottom summary places those sample centers on a common scale so their separation can be judged together.","Changing the positions of group centers changes the between-group picture. The individual scatter within a group continues to describe the variation among people receiving that same treatment.","A large-looking difference must be interpreted relative to that individual scatter and the group size. The comparison asks whether ordinary sampling from one population can plausibly produce the observed arrangement of centers."]},"6:8":{"title":"Why means have less spread than individuals","paragraphs":["The individual measurement follows a population distribution with variance \\(\\sigma^2\\). An average of several independent measurements has a smaller variance because the individual deviations partly cancel.","The distribution of sample means remains centered at the population mean. Its variance is the individual variance divided by the number of observations averaged.","This relationship lets ANOVA work backward. If the variance of the group means reflects sampling alone, multiplying it by the group size should recover an estimate of the original population variance."]},"6:9":{"title":"Estimating the same population variance two ways","paragraphs":["Under the null hypothesis, the control and three diet groups share a population mean. The setup also assumes a common population variance for the cardiac-output measurements.","Within each group, the scatter of individuals estimates that common variance. Across groups, the scatter of their sample means also contains information about it once the averaging effect is accounted for.","If all groups really come from the same population, these two estimates should be of similar size. A treatment-related separation of population means makes the between-group estimate grow relative to the within-group estimate."]},"6:10":{"title":"The within-groups variance estimate","paragraphs":["First calculate each group's sample mean and variance using its seven individuals. Every group's variance measures spread around its own mean.","Because the groups have equal sizes, average their variances to obtain the within-groups estimate. Each group contributes equally to this shared estimate of individual variation.","This calculation does not use the distances between group means. It deliberately measures background scatter inside the groups, providing the reference against which the between-group variation will be compared."]},"6:11":{"title":"Returning to the cardiac-output picture","paragraphs":["The four marked sample means show the observed centers of the diet groups. Their individual standard deviations describe the spread around those centers.","The difference between centers supplies the between-group information. The spread within each group supplies the within-group information, so both parts can be read from the same set of measurements.","The comparison is about relative size. If the centers are only modestly separated compared with the individual scatter, their differences can be compatible with random sampling from equal-mean populations."]},"6:12":{"title":"Converting mean variation into population variance","paragraphs":["Treat the four group means as a small set of values and calculate their variance around the grand mean. The grand mean is the average of these equally sized groups' means.","That variance describes the scatter of averages, not the scatter of individuals. Averages based on seven people are expected to have one seventh of the population variance.","Multiply the variance of the group means by seven to put the between-groups estimate on the same scale as the within-groups estimate. Equal group sizes are essential for this particular shortcut."]},"6:13":{"title":"The F statistic compares the two estimates","paragraphs":["Place the between-groups variance estimate in the numerator and the within-groups estimate in the denominator. Both estimate the same population variance when the null hypothesis is true.","A ratio near one means the two estimates are comparable. A large ratio means the group means are much more separated than expected from the within-group variation.","F changes across repeated samples, so it has its own probability distribution. This distribution determines how large a ratio counts as unusually large."]},"6:14":{"title":"A critical F value marks the upper tail","paragraphs":["The critical value is chosen so that a specified small portion of the null F distribution lies to its right. The example uses an upper-tail probability of 0.05.","If the observed F exceeds that boundary, it falls in the region designated for rejecting equal population means. The boundary depends on the group counts and sample sizes through degrees of freedom.","The tail area is calculated under the null hypothesis. It describes how unusual a statistic would be if the population means were equal, rather than the probability that a particular conclusion is true."]},"6:15":{"title":"Comparing within-group and between-group patterns","paragraphs":["The illustration labels variation within groups as A and variation between groups as B. Within-group variation describes the ordinary differences among individuals sharing a group.","Between-group variation describes the separation of the group centers. When B is large relative to A, the group centers look too separated to attribute comfortably to sampling alone.","ANOVA's overall conclusion concerns whether all population means are equal. Evidence of a difference does not yet identify which particular pair of groups differs; that is a later comparison task."]},"6:16":{"title":"Cell-phone use and sperm motility","paragraphs":["The groups represent different levels of cell-phone use, and the measured outcome is sperm motility. Each group contains the same number of subjects, allowing the balanced ANOVA calculation.","The table provides a mean and standard deviation for each group. Means retain percentage units, while squaring a standard deviation produces variance in squared percentage units.","The null hypothesis says the population mean motility is the same across usage groups. Read the group labels as the comparison conditions and the motility values as the numerical response being tested."]},"6:17":{"title":"Building the two motility variance estimates","paragraphs":["Average the four group variances to describe variation among individuals within usage groups. Square the reported standard deviations before averaging them.","Next calculate how far each group mean is from the grand mean and use those squared distances to obtain the variance of the means. Multiply that result by the common group size.","The resulting between-groups quantity is much larger than the variance of the means itself because it has been rescaled to the individual-variance scale. Only after that rescaling should it be compared with the within-groups estimate."]},"6:18":{"title":"Deciding the motility ANOVA result","paragraphs":["The F ratio uses the calculated between-groups and within-groups estimates. Its numerator degrees of freedom come from the four group means, while the denominator comes from variation within all four groups.","The table may not list the exact denominator degrees of freedom. The displayed neighboring rows bound the relevant critical values, allowing comparison without pretending an unavailable table entry is printed.","The observed statistic exceeds the displayed threshold even at the stricter significance level. Reject equal population means and conclude that sperm motility differs somewhere among the usage groups."]},"6:19":{"title":"Burnout scores across clinical units","paragraphs":["The study measures burnout among health professionals from four clinical units. Unit membership defines the groups, and the questionnaire score is the numerical outcome.","The question is whether the population mean burnout score differs across those units. Start by identifying each group's size, mean, and standard deviation in the table.","The ANOVA comparison uses separation among unit means relative to variability among professionals within units. Keep the outcome as burnout score; the proportions of patients with HIV describe the clinical settings rather than replacing the measured response."]},"6:20":{"title":"Where the ANOVA framework extends","paragraphs":["ANOVA relies on assumptions about the data and their sampling, including the variation within populations. Those assumptions determine when the reference F distribution is appropriate.","Unequal group sizes require a formulation that accounts for different numbers of observations. Two-way ANOVA introduces more than one grouping factor, while repeated-measures ANOVA addresses measurements linked within subjects.","These headings describe extensions of the method just developed. The balanced one-way calculation remains the immediate framework: one numerical response compared across independently sampled groups of equal size."]},"7:1":{"title":"ANOVA from a method to complete examples","paragraphs":["This lecture consolidates the balanced ANOVA procedure and applies it to heart rate and lung function. In each application, identify the numerical response and the groups before calculating.","The sum-of-squares framework expresses the same within-versus-between comparison in another notation. It also accommodates unequal group sizes, although hand calculation of that framework is outside this midterm.","The R section shows how group and residual rows represent the two sources of variation. Reading those rows connects software output to the variance comparison already developed by hand."]},"7:2":{"title":"The equal-means population picture","paragraphs":["The diet labels are mixed throughout one common cardiac-output distribution. The full hypothetical population therefore illustrates the null case in which diet does not shift the population mean.","Individual measurements still vary. Equal population means do not mean every individual has the same cardiac output.","This distinction explains why observed samples can show different averages under the null. The test asks whether that sample separation is excessive relative to the variation already present among individuals."]},"7:3":{"title":"What the sampled diet groups show","paragraphs":["The sample means mark the centers of four small diet groups, and the standard deviations summarize the spread inside those groups. Some separation of sample means is expected from random selection.","The central question compares that separation with the scatter among individuals. Large individual scatter makes modest differences between group means less surprising.","The within-groups and between-groups estimates turn those two visual features into comparable quantities. Neither a gap between two means nor a wide individual range alone supplies the full ANOVA answer."]},"7:4":{"title":"Comparing group centers on a shared scale","paragraphs":["The bottom summary brings the group means together, while the upper rows retain the individual observations. This separates the variability of averages from the variability of individual people.","Averages based on several independent observations should fluctuate less than individual observations. The expected reduction depends on how many individuals contribute to each mean.","The picture asks whether the observed scatter of means agrees with that expected sampling scatter. The F ratio will be large when the centers are separated beyond what the within-group variation predicts."]},"7:5":{"title":"Begin with the hypotheses and group summaries","paragraphs":["The null hypothesis states that all population group means are equal. The alternative requires at least one pair of population means to differ, rather than requiring every group to differ from every other group.","Calculate each sample's mean and variance. The mean describes its center, and the variance describes individual scatter around that group's own center.","Keep \\(m\\) (number of groups) separate from \\(n\\) (subjects per group). Both enter the balanced calculation, but they count different things."]},"7:6":{"title":"Put within-group and between-group variation on one scale","paragraphs":["Average the equally sized groups' variances to obtain the within-groups estimate. Then average the group means to obtain the grand mean.","Calculate the variance of the group means around that grand mean. This quantity measures the scatter of averages, so it is smaller in scale than the variance of individuals under the null.","Multiply it by the common group size to obtain the between-groups estimate of population variance. The two estimates are now ready to form a meaningful ratio."]},"7:7":{"title":"Finish ANOVA with F and its degrees of freedom","paragraphs":["Divide the between-groups estimate by the within-groups estimate. The result measures how large the group separation is relative to the background scatter.","The numerator degrees of freedom equal the number of groups minus one. The denominator degrees of freedom add the within-group contributions across all groups.","Use those two degrees of freedom and the significance level to find the upper-tail F critical value. Reject equal population means only when the observed ratio exceeds that boundary."]},"7:8":{"title":"Conditions behind the ANOVA comparison","paragraphs":["The response must be numerical interval data, and observations must come from independent, randomly selected samples. Independence concerns how observations are obtained, rather than whether the sample means happen to be close.","The method assumes Normal populations with equal population variances. Equal variances provide the common background spread that both variance estimates are trying to measure.","The upper-tail F picture applies under these conditions and the null hypothesis. Keep the assumptions attached to the method before interpreting its calculated ratio."]},"7:9":{"title":"Set up the cat-versus-dog heart-rate comparison","paragraphs":["Five students are sampled from each pet-preference group. The measured response is heart rate, and the null hypothesis says the two population mean heart rates are equal.","The sample means are 74.2 and 76.8 beats per minute. The reported standard deviations must be squared before they enter the within-groups variance calculation.","The small difference between means is the feature being tested. Its importance depends on how large it is relative to within-group scatter and the sample sizes."]},"7:10":{"title":"Heart-rate ANOVA: within-group variance","paragraphs":["This step averages the squared standard deviations of the two equally sized groups. The result, 68.05, estimates the shared variation among individual heart rates.","Use each group's spread around its own mean. Do not include the difference between the two group means in this within-group calculation.","The result is in squared beats-per-minute units. It will become the denominator of F, supplying the background variation against which the separation of the means is judged."]},"7:11":{"title":"Heart-rate ANOVA: the grand mean","paragraphs":["Average the two sample means because both groups contain the same number of students. The grand mean is 75.5 beats per minute.","This value is the center of the group means. It supplies the reference point for measuring how far each group's mean lies from the overall center.","The grand mean is not an extra sample and does not add more observations. It is a summary calculated from the existing groups before their between-group variation is measured."]},"7:12":{"title":"Heart-rate ANOVA: variance of the means","paragraphs":["Subtract the grand mean from each group mean. One group lies below it and the other lies above it, with equal distances in this two-group example.","Square the distances, add them, and divide by the number of group means minus one. The resulting 3.38 describes variation among the means.","This is not yet the between-groups estimate on the individual-variance scale. The next step accounts for the fact that each displayed mean averages five students."]},"7:13":{"title":"Heart-rate ANOVA: rescale by group size","paragraphs":["Multiply the variance of the two group means by five, the number of students contributing to each mean. The between-groups variance estimate becomes 16.9.","The multiplication reverses the usual reduction in variability caused by averaging. It lets this estimate be compared directly with the within-groups estimate of 68.05.","Both quantities now estimate individual population variance under the null hypothesis. Their ratio will reveal whether the observed mean separation is large compared with the within-group variation."]},"7:14":{"title":"Heart-rate ANOVA: organize the decision","paragraphs":["The calculation now moves from the two variance estimates to their ratio. Place the between-groups estimate above the within-groups estimate.","Next select a critical F value using the number of groups and the number of subjects per group. The significance level specifies the small upper-tail region used for rejection.","The decision requires comparing the statistic with that boundary. Keep these as distinct steps: calculate what the data produced, find what the null reference allows, then state the conclusion."]},"7:15":{"title":"Heart-rate ANOVA: calculate F and degrees of freedom","paragraphs":["Dividing 16.9 by 68.05 gives an F value near 0.248. The between-groups estimate is smaller than the within-groups estimate in this sample.","Two groups give one numerator degree of freedom. Two groups of five give eight denominator degrees of freedom.","Those degrees of freedom select the relevant F reference distribution. The statistic is not interpreted as a probability; it must be compared with a table boundary for that distribution."]},"7:16":{"title":"Heart-rate ANOVA: find the table cutoff","paragraphs":["For numerator degrees of freedom one and denominator degrees of freedom eight, the displayed 5% critical F value is 5.32. This marks the upper-tail rejection boundary.","The observed F is about 0.248, far below that boundary. The ratio therefore does not fall into the unusually large region specified by the test.","The table lookup depends on both degrees of freedom. Reversing their roles would select a different distribution and could give the wrong decision boundary."]},"7:17":{"title":"Heart-rate ANOVA: state the result","paragraphs":["The observed F does not exceed the critical value, so do not reject the equal-population-means hypothesis. These sample data do not establish a heart-rate difference between the pet-preference groups.","The sample averages are still different numerically. The test says that this separation is not sufficiently large relative to the observed within-group variation.","State the conclusion in terms of the measured response and groups. The outcome is heart rate among cat-preferring and dog-preferring students, rather than a general statement about pet preference."]},"7:18":{"title":"Lung function and tobacco exposure","paragraphs":["Forced mid-expiratory flow measures how rapidly air leaves the lungs during part of a forced expiration. Reduced flow is associated with small-airways disease in this example.","Five groups represent different tobacco-pollutant exposures, with the same number of subjects in each. The null hypothesis says their population mean lung-function measurements are equal.","This is another balanced one-way ANOVA setup. The biological meaning changes from heart rate to lung flow, but the comparison still weighs differences among group means against variation within groups."]},"7:19":{"title":"Lung-function ANOVA: background variation","paragraphs":["Square each group's standard deviation and average the five variances. The highlighted within-groups estimate is 0.52 in squared flow units.","This quantity describes individual variation within exposure groups. It is calculated separately from any separation between their average lung-function values.","Because every group has the same size, the simple average of variances is appropriate for this formulation. It supplies the denominator when the F ratio is assembled."]},"7:20":{"title":"Lung-function ANOVA: locate the overall center","paragraphs":["The highlighted calculation averages the five group means to obtain a grand mean of 2.59 liters per second. Equal group sizes give each mean the same weight.","That overall center is the reference for the next calculation. Each group's mean will be compared with 2.59, rather than with another group's mean alone.","The grand mean remains in the original flow units. Squared units appear only when the distances from that center are squared to describe variation."]},"7:21":{"title":"Lung-function ANOVA: quantify the mean separation","paragraphs":["Subtract the grand mean from each of the five group means. Square those distances, add them, and divide by four to calculate the variance of the means.","The highlighted result summarizes how widely the exposure-group averages are spread. It concerns five means, each of which summarizes many individuals.","That distinction explains the next multiplication by group size. The variance of means must be rescaled before it can be compared with the within-groups estimate of individual variation."]},"7:22":{"title":"Lung-function ANOVA: obtain the between-groups estimate","paragraphs":["Multiply the variance of the group means by the common group size of 200. The highlighted between-groups estimate is approximately 33.3.","This estimate is now on the same squared-flow scale as the within-groups estimate. Both would estimate a common population variance if the population means were equal.","Here the between-groups estimate is much larger than the background within-group estimate. That contrast is what produces a large F statistic in the next step."]},"7:23":{"title":"Lung-function ANOVA: move from estimates to a test","paragraphs":["The two variance estimates have been assembled, so divide between-group variation by within-group variation. This expresses their contrast as one dimensionless F ratio.","The group count and group size determine the reference distribution. They supply separate numerator and denominator degrees of freedom for the table lookup.","Finally compare the ratio with the chosen upper-tail boundary. This page organizes the decision sequence rather than introducing a different way to measure lung function."]},"7:24":{"title":"Lung-function ANOVA: compare with bounded table values","paragraphs":["The calculated F is about 64.18, with four numerator and 995 denominator degrees of freedom. Its size reflects the large between-group estimate relative to the within-group estimate.","The exact denominator row is not displayed in the table, so the slide uses nearby tabulated bounds. Both relevant critical values are only a little above three at the stated level.","The observed statistic exceeds either bound by a wide margin. An exact interpolated cutoff is unnecessary for deciding which side of the rejection boundary this result occupies."]},"7:25":{"title":"Lung-function ANOVA: interpret rejection","paragraphs":["Reject the null hypothesis of equal population means for the tobacco-exposure groups. The displayed comparison supports a lung-function difference somewhere among those groups.","ANOVA combines information across all groups into one overall test. Its rejection does not by itself identify which exposure pairs differ.","Keep the response in the conclusion: forced expiratory flow differs among the groups at the stated significance level. Pairwise follow-up methods later isolate particular differences."]},"7:26":{"title":"Two subscripts identify group and subject","paragraphs":["The first subscript identifies the treatment group, and the second identifies a person within that group. This lets each measurement retain both pieces of information.","A separate group-size symbol allows different groups to contain different numbers of subjects. The total sample size then combines those group counts.","This notation prepares the sum-of-squares formulation. It describes the same measured data more explicitly, without changing what within-group variation or between-group separation means."]},"7:27":{"title":"From group variance to a sum of squared deviations","paragraphs":["For each group, calculate its mean, then subtract that mean from every observation in the group. Squaring and adding those distances gives the group's sum of squares.","Unlike a sample variance, a sum of squares has not yet been divided by degrees of freedom. It preserves the total squared variation contributed by that group.","Keeping these totals allows groups of different sizes to contribute appropriately. The group means still provide the centers around which within-group distances are measured."]},"7:28":{"title":"Combining within-group sums of squares","paragraphs":["Add the individual groups' sums of squares to obtain the total within-groups sum of squares. Every observation contributes through its distance from its own group mean.","Divide that total by the within-groups degrees of freedom to get the within-groups mean square. This mean square is another notation for the within-groups variance estimate.","The denominator uses total subjects minus groups because one mean has been estimated within each group. Read sum of squares as total squared variation and mean square as variation adjusted for its degrees of freedom."]},"7:29":{"title":"Between-group sums of squares and mean square","paragraphs":["The between-groups calculation uses the distances of group means from the grand mean. Group size weights those distances so that a larger group contributes according to its number of observations.","Dividing the resulting sum of squares by the number of groups minus one gives the between-groups mean square. That is the numerator variance estimate in ANOVA.","The compact expression shown is an algebraic way to assemble the same quantity. Keep its purpose visible: measuring group-center separation after accounting for how many observations each center represents."]},"7:30":{"title":"F from mean squares","paragraphs":["Divide the between-groups mean square by the within-groups mean square. This is the same F comparison used in the balanced variance formulation.","The numerator degrees of freedom are the between-groups degrees of freedom. The denominator degrees of freedom are total observations minus the number of groups.","Use the F table to compare the resulting statistic with its upper-tail critical value. A new notation for the components does not change the direction of the rejection rule."]},"7:31":{"title":"Reading the complete squared-error framework","paragraphs":["The left side gathers variation within groups, and the right side gathers variation between group centers. Each sum of squares is divided by its own degrees of freedom.","Those divisions produce the within and between mean squares. Their ratio forms F, with the same degrees of freedom carried into the reference distribution.","Follow each quantity's role rather than treating the page as disconnected formulas. Observations produce squared deviations, squared deviations produce mean squares, and the ratio compares the two sources of variation."]},"7:32":{"title":"Cardiac-output observations for the sum-of-squares example","paragraphs":["The table lists individual cardiac-output measurements under the four diet conditions. Each column belongs to one treatment group and contains seven observations.","The group mean supplies the center for that column's deviations. Subtract it from each cardiac-output value, square the differences, and add them to obtain that group's sum of squares.","The bottom summaries gather the ingredients for the next pages. Keep the individual measurements, group means, and sums of squares distinct: they describe raw outcomes, centers, and total squared variation."]},"7:33":{"title":"Cardiac-output within-groups mean square","paragraphs":["Adding the four group sums of squares gives 3.825. This total measures variation of people around their own diet group's mean.","There are 28 observations and four fitted group means, leaving 24 within-groups degrees of freedom. Dividing the total by 24 gives the displayed within-groups variance estimate near 0.159.","This is the denominator component of F. The division changes a total sum of squared deviations into an estimate of individual population variance."]},"7:34":{"title":"Cardiac-output between-groups mean square","paragraphs":["The between-groups sum of squares measures how far the diet means lie from the grand mean, accounting for the seven people behind each mean. The displayed total is about 0.685.","Four groups give three between-groups degrees of freedom. Dividing by three produces a mean square near 0.228.","This is the numerator component of F. Compare it with the within-groups mean square only after both sums of squares have been divided by their appropriate degrees of freedom."]},"7:35":{"title":"Cardiac-output F and conclusion","paragraphs":["The ratio of the displayed mean squares is about 1.43. The relevant F distribution uses three numerator and 24 denominator degrees of freedom.","The 5% critical value is about 3.01, so the observed ratio does not reach the rejection region. Do not reject equal population mean cardiac output for these diets.","The sample means can differ without producing a significant overall result. Here their separation is modest compared with the within-group variation represented in the denominator."]},"7:36":{"title":"Choosing an ANOVA formulation from available information","paragraphs":["Unequal group sizes require a formulation that accounts for those different sizes. The sum-of-squares approach does so using individual observations.","With equal group sizes and raw measurements, either formulation can describe the same ANOVA comparison. With equal group sizes but only group means and standard deviations, use the variance-comparison approach.","Start by checking what the question supplies. For this midterm's balanced calculations, the summary means, standard deviations, and common group size provide the ingredients for the variance method."]},"7:37":{"title":"Exhaled nitric oxide across lung-function groups","paragraphs":["The observations are exhaled nitric-oxide measurements from young asthmatic subjects after allergen exposure. Lung-function classifications divide the subjects into three groups.","The question asks whether those groups share the same population mean nitric-oxide level. The raw table shows that the numbers of subjects are not identical across columns.","Identify the response and grouping factor before interpreting the analysis. The next R example summarizes the between-group and within-group variation for these measurements without requiring a hand calculation of every squared deviation."]},"7:38":{"title":"Reading an ANOVA formula in R","paragraphs":["The response is the numerical outcome being analyzed. The factor identifies the categories that divide observations into comparison groups.","The formula places the response on the left and the grouping factor on the right. The data argument identifies the data frame where both variables are stored.","This syntax describes the statistical question: does the response vary across the factor's groups? Understanding those roles makes it easier to interpret the resulting table, even when the exam does not ask you to write commands."]},"7:39":{"title":"Interpreting the nitric-oxide ANOVA output","paragraphs":["The Group row describes between-group variation, and the Residuals row describes within-group variation. Dividing the group mean square by the residual mean square produces the reported F value.","The output gives two numerator and 19 denominator degrees of freedom. Its p value is about 0.0106, which is below 0.05 but slightly above 0.01.","At the 5% level, reject equal population mean nitric-oxide levels. Keep the exact p-value comparison attached to the chosen significance level, and use the residual mean square if a follow-up calculation needs the within-groups variance."]},"8:1":{"title":"Two groups and one reference value","paragraphs":["With two independent groups, the t test compares the difference between sample means with the uncertainty in that difference. Larger sample scatter makes the same observed gap less convincing.","The fasting-glucose and anesthetic examples apply this comparison to different biomedical measurements. The ingredients remain group means, group spreads, and sample sizes.","A one-sample t test instead compares one sample mean with a fixed reference value. Identify whether the question supplies a second sampled group or a single benchmark before choosing the calculation."]},"8:2":{"title":"Using Homework 1 to connect the methods","paragraphs":["The discussion is organized around explaining Homework 1 and resolving questions about the material. Start each problem by identifying what is random and what quantity the question asks for.","A probability question may ask for an area, a cutoff, an expectation, or a variance. Those answers have different meanings even when they begin with the same distribution.","Keep the given values beside their labels while working. That makes it easier to connect a homework problem to the appropriate course method without treating every expression as a new formula to memorize."]},"8:3":{"title":"Why a larger sample clarifies the same group difference","paragraphs":["The diagrams compare urine production in placebo and drug groups, first with five subjects per group and then with twenty. The average separation can look similar while its uncertainty changes.","A sample mean becomes more stable as more independent individuals contribute to it. The standard error shrinks with the square root of sample size, even when individual urine-production values remain scattered.","Thus sample size changes how precisely the group centers are estimated. The t test uses that precision when judging whether an observed mean difference is unusually large."]},"8:4":{"title":"Difference divided by uncertainty in the difference","paragraphs":["The numerator is the observed gap between the two sample means. The denominator is the standard error of that gap, describing how much the difference would fluctuate across repeated sampling.","A larger gap increases the magnitude of t. A larger standard error decreases it because a given difference is less surprising when sample differences naturally fluctuate more.","The sign records the chosen group order. For a two-sided test, evidence depends on the magnitude of the separation, so compare the absolute t value with the positive critical value."]},"8:5":{"title":"Combining the uncertainty from two sample means","paragraphs":["Each sample mean has its own standard error, obtained from its group spread and sample size. The difference between two means depends on both uncertain estimates.","For independent groups, the variances of the two means add to give the variance of their difference. Take a square root only after adding those variance contributions.","This is why the denominator is not merely the difference between the groups' standard deviations. It describes uncertainty in a difference of averages, with both sample sizes included."]},"8:6":{"title":"Means of sums and differences","paragraphs":["When two variables are added, their expected values add. When one is subtracted from the other, the corresponding expected value is subtracted too.","The integral illustration separates each variable's contribution. Integrating the other variable's density gives one, leaving the appropriate original mean.","For a group comparison, the expected difference of sample means is the difference of the population means. Under the equal-means null hypothesis, that expected difference is zero."]},"8:7":{"title":"Why variances add even for a difference","paragraphs":["A difference between independent variables still receives random variation from both of them. One variable may move the difference upward while the other moves it downward, but both add uncertainty.","In the squared-deviation calculation, the subtraction sign is squared. Independence removes the cross-dependence term, leaving the sum of the two variance contributions.","Thus subtracting means and combining uncertainty follow different rules. The centers subtract, while the independent variance contributions add before a standard deviation is obtained."]},"8:8":{"title":"Pooling the two groups' variance estimates","paragraphs":["The denominator begins with the variance of each sample mean. Under the equal-population-variance model, both groups estimate the same underlying individual variance.","A pooled variance combines their estimates, weighting each by its degrees of freedom. The larger group contributes more information rather than receiving equal weight automatically.","Substitute that shared estimate into the standard error of the mean difference. This is the pooled two-sample t procedure taught here, with a denominator based on both sample sizes."]},"8:9":{"title":"Set up the independent two-sample t test","paragraphs":["There are two treatment groups made up of different individuals. The null hypothesis states that their population means are equal.","Compute or identify each group's sample mean, sample variance, and sample size. These quantities describe the observed center, individual variation, and amount of information in each group.","The sample means provide the observed difference. The variances and sizes provide its estimated uncertainty, which is why none of those ingredients should be omitted when assembling the test."]},"8:10":{"title":"Calculate pooled variance, then t","paragraphs":["Weight each sample variance by its sample size minus one, add those weighted contributions, and divide by the combined degrees of freedom. This produces the pooled individual-variance estimate.","Use it with the two reciprocal sample sizes to calculate the standard error of the difference. Divide the mean difference by that standard error to obtain t.","The degrees of freedom equal the two sample sizes added together, minus two. Carry that value into the table lookup rather than using either group's sample size alone."]},"8:11":{"title":"Two-sided decision and t-test conditions","paragraphs":["The two-sided test checks for differences in either direction. Compare the absolute observed t with the critical value from the two-tailed table.","The pooled procedure assumes independent random samples from Normal populations with equal variances, measured on a numerical interval scale. These conditions support its particular denominator and reference distribution.","If the magnitude exceeds the table cutoff, reject equal population means at the chosen significance level. State which measured outcome differs between which groups rather than reporting a bare rejection."]},"8:12":{"title":"Why the rejection region has two tails","paragraphs":["Repeated samples under equal population means produce positive and negative t values around zero. Large positive or negative values both represent a large separation from the null expectation.","The shaded outer regions mark unusually large magnitudes. A stricter significance level moves the boundaries farther from zero, requiring stronger observed separation for rejection.","The absolute-value comparison combines those two tails into one rule. It lets a negative observed statistic be evaluated against the same positive critical magnitude as a positive statistic."]},"8:13":{"title":"Reading the two-tailed t table","paragraphs":["Choose the row for the test's degrees of freedom and the column for the desired total two-tailed significance level. Their intersection gives the critical magnitude.","Small degrees of freedom require larger cutoffs because estimating spread from limited data adds uncertainty. As degrees of freedom increase, the critical values approach the familiar Normal cutoffs.","Check the table heading before selecting a column. A two-tailed probability is shared across both extreme tails, so it must not be confused with the probability in one tail alone."]},"8:14":{"title":"Gosset and Student's t","paragraphs":["William Sealy Gosset published the work shown under the name Student. The article concerns uncertainty in an estimated mean, which is the central problem behind the t distribution.","With a small sample, the population spread is usually unknown and must be estimated from the same data. That estimated spread varies across samples and adds uncertainty to the standardized mean.","The t reference accommodates that extra uncertainty through its degrees of freedom. The historical page connects the name Student's t with this practical problem of learning about a mean from limited observations."]},"8:15":{"title":"Fasting glucose in two groups of children","paragraphs":["The comparison is between children with and without a diabetic parent. Each plotted point is a child's fasting-glucose measurement, and each group has 25 observations.","The group with a diabetic parent has the higher sample mean. The standard deviations describe the scatter of children around their respective group means.","The statistical question asks whether that separation is large compared with its sampling uncertainty. Equal sample sizes simplify the pooled variance, but the test still needs both groups' spreads and means."]},"8:16":{"title":"Completing the fasting-glucose test","paragraphs":["The pooled variance combines the two group variances, then the standard error accounts for the two sample sizes. Dividing the mean gap by that error gives a t magnitude near six.","The combined sample sizes produce 48 degrees of freedom. The statistic exceeds even the displayed critical magnitude for a two-tailed significance level of 0.001.","Reject equal population mean fasting glucose for these groups. The source's displayed t is rounded; the conclusion is unchanged when the reported means and standard deviations are calculated without early rounding."]},"8:17":{"title":"Choosing cardiac index from the anesthetic table","paragraphs":["The table compares halothane and morphine groups and reports several cardiovascular measurements. The highlighted response for this example is cardiac index.","The groups have different sample sizes, so pool their variances using degrees-of-freedom weights. Use the cardiac-index mean and standard deviation from each column, rather than mixing rows for different physiological outcomes.","The null hypothesis concerns equal population mean cardiac index under the two anesthetic conditions. Blood pressure and peripheral resistance are separate outcomes in the same table."]},"8:18":{"title":"Organizing the halothane-versus-morphine calculation","paragraphs":["Fill in pooled variance first using the cardiac-index standard deviations and sample sizes. That establishes the shared background variation for this response.","Next calculate the standard error of the difference and the observed t. Obtain degrees of freedom by adding the two sample sizes and subtracting two.","Finally select the two-tailed critical value and compare magnitudes. The blanks separate the data calculation from the reference-table decision so each ingredient can be checked independently."]},"8:19":{"title":"The cardiac-index difference is not significant","paragraphs":["The pooled cardiac-index variance is about 0.889, producing a t magnitude around 0.840. The reference distribution has 23 degrees of freedom.","The 5% two-tailed critical magnitude is about 2.069. The observed statistic is smaller, so do not reject equal population mean cardiac index.","The sample means differ numerically, but the gap is small relative to its estimated uncertainty. The conclusion concerns cardiac index for these anesthetic groups, not every cardiovascular quantity listed in the earlier table."]},"8:20":{"title":"One sample compared with a fixed benchmark","paragraphs":["A one-sample t test has one group of measured individuals and one specified reference mean. The benchmark is not a second sample with its own estimated spread.","Calculate the sample mean and sample variance from the group. The null hypothesis states that its population mean equals the reference value.","The numerator will compare the observed mean with that fixed benchmark. Its denominator therefore uses uncertainty in this one sample mean rather than combining two sampled groups."]},"8:21":{"title":"One-sample t and its reference distribution","paragraphs":["Subtract the reference mean from the sample mean, then divide by the estimated standard error of the sample mean. The result measures the observed discrepancy in standard-error units.","One estimated sample mean leaves sample size minus one degrees of freedom for the t reference. Use that row in the two-tailed table.","Compare the absolute statistic with the critical magnitude. A difference in either direction can count as evidence against the specified population mean."]},"8:22":{"title":"Heart rate compared with 72 beats per minute","paragraphs":["Five students provide a sample mean of 76.8 beats per minute. Their sample standard deviation is 4.15, while 72 is the fixed population-mean value being tested.","The null states that the population mean heart rate equals 72; the alternative allows it to differ in either direction. The observed mean is above the benchmark.","The next calculation asks whether that upward discrepancy is large relative to the uncertainty in a mean based on only five students. Keep the sample standard deviation separate from the smaller standard error."]},"8:23":{"title":"Calculate the one-sample heart-rate statistic","paragraphs":["The numerator is the sample mean minus 72, giving a discrepancy of 4.8 beats per minute. The denominator is the sample standard deviation divided by the square root of five.","That standard error is about 1.856 beats per minute. Dividing the discrepancy by it gives t near 2.586.","Five observations give four degrees of freedom. The units cancel in the ratio, leaving a statistic that can be compared with a t-table critical value."]},"8:24":{"title":"Finish the one-sample heart-rate decision","paragraphs":["For four degrees of freedom, the two-tailed 5% critical magnitude is about 2.776. The observed magnitude, 2.586, is slightly smaller.","Do not reject the hypothesis that the population mean heart rate is 72 beats per minute. The sample's higher average does not cross this test's rejection boundary.","This example shows why the table step matters. A t value greater than two is not automatically significant when the degrees of freedom are small."]},"9:1":{"title":"Finding differences after an overall comparison","paragraphs":["ANOVA can show that the population means are not all equal, but it does not identify the differing pairs. Follow-up comparisons address those more specific questions.","Testing many pairs creates more opportunities for a false positive. Bonferroni and Holm adjust the comparison thresholds to control error across the set of tests.","The p value describes how extreme a statistic would be under the null model. Keep that reference condition attached: it is not the probability that the null hypothesis itself is true."]},"9:2":{"title":"What the overall ANOVA establishes","paragraphs":["The F statistic compares the between-groups and within-groups variance estimates. Its reference distribution uses separate numerator and denominator degrees of freedom.","A sufficiently large F rejects the hypothesis that all population means are equal. That conclusion establishes an overall difference somewhere in the group set.","The result does not specify which pair supplies the difference. The next stage uses the within-groups estimate to examine selected pairs while controlling the error from multiple comparisons."]},"9:3":{"title":"Why repeated ordinary t tests need care","paragraphs":["A two-group t test compares a mean difference with its standard error. Used once at a chosen significance level, it has the corresponding null rejection rule.","Applying that same rule separately to many pairs creates a family of decisions. More decisions give more chances to label a difference significant when the relevant null is true.","A multiple-comparison procedure changes the thresholds for that family. The issue is the collection of tests and its error control, rather than a change in the meaning of an individual mean difference."]},"9:4":{"title":"Three separate pairwise tests on three groups","paragraphs":["The example compares placebo, testosterone, and estrogen using separate ordinary t tests. Only the placebo-versus-testosterone statistic exceeds the displayed unadjusted cutoff.","That pattern does not make significance transitive: a nonsignificant result for two other pairs does not prove those population means are equal. The sample comparisons have their own uncertainty.","The central problem is using unadjusted tests across a family of comparisons. The later procedures use a common ANOVA error estimate and adjusted thresholds instead of treating every pair as an isolated test."]},"9:5":{"title":"Reusing ANOVA's within-groups variance","paragraphs":["After ANOVA, use its within-groups variance estimate in the standard error for each pair of means. This pools background information across the original groups.","The degrees of freedom stay attached to that estimate. Use the ANOVA residual degrees of freedom, rather than calculating a new two-group denominator for each selected pair.","With equal group sizes, the standard-error expression simplifies. With different sizes, each selected group's reciprocal sample size remains in the expression, but the shared error estimate still comes from ANOVA."]},"9:6":{"title":"Bonferroni divides the error allowance","paragraphs":["Choose an overall significance level for the complete family of comparisons. Divide it by the number of comparisons to obtain the level used for each individual test.","More comparisons produce a smaller per-test level and therefore a larger critical t magnitude. The evidence required for each pair becomes stricter.","The mean differences and their standard errors are calculated as before using the ANOVA error estimate. Bonferroni changes the decision threshold, rather than shrinking or enlarging the observed differences."]},"9:7":{"title":"Activity groups and HDL: the overall result","paragraphs":["The groups are marathon runners, joggers, and inactive men, with HDL concentration as the measured response. Each group has seventy subjects.","Their sample means are separated, and the overall ANOVA gives a large F relative to its reference boundary. The equal-population-means null is rejected.","The within-groups variance and its 207 degrees of freedom will support the follow-up tests. The task now changes from asking whether any difference exists to identifying which activity-group pairs differ."]},"9:8":{"title":"Bonferroni for all three HDL pairs","paragraphs":["Three groups produce three distinct pairwise comparisons. Dividing the overall error allowance among those three gives one shared adjusted significance level.","Each mean gap is divided by the standard error based on the ANOVA within-groups variance. Because the group sizes match, the same denominator applies to all three gaps.","All three observed t magnitudes exceed the adjusted critical value. The results support differences between runners and joggers, runners and inactive men, and joggers and inactive men under this familywise procedure."]},"9:9":{"title":"Holm tests the strongest evidence first","paragraphs":["Holm begins with the most significant comparison, which has the largest absolute t when all comparisons share the same reference degrees of freedom. It uses the strictest threshold first.","If that comparison passes, the next comparison uses a slightly larger significance level. The allowed level increases as fewer hypotheses remain to be considered.","Stop at the first comparison that fails its threshold and do not reject the remaining hypotheses. This ordered rule is essential; the comparisons cannot be assigned thresholds in an arbitrary original order."]},"9:10":{"title":"Reuse the same HDL ANOVA for Holm","paragraphs":["The HDL group means, standard deviations, and sample sizes have not changed. The overall ANOVA still rejects equal population means.","Its within-groups estimate and residual degrees of freedom remain the basis for the follow-up t statistics. Switching to Holm does not require recomputing the group data with a new background variance.","What changes is the sequence of thresholds. Arrange the pairwise evidence from strongest to weakest before deciding which significance level applies to each pair."]},"9:11":{"title":"Holm across the three HDL comparisons","paragraphs":["The runner-versus-inactive gap gives the largest t magnitude, followed by jogger-versus-inactive and then runner-versus-jogger. This is the order used for Holm's steps.","The first comparison uses the most restrictive level. Each successful rejection permits the next comparison to use a less restrictive threshold while retaining the same residual degrees of freedom.","All three comparisons pass their respective thresholds in this example. The last comparison faces a smaller critical magnitude than under Bonferroni because Holm has already dealt with the stronger comparisons."]},"9:12":{"title":"Bonferroni when only controls are compared","paragraphs":["A control-comparison family contains only the selected treatment-versus-control pairs. It does not automatically include every possible pair among the groups.","Count the comparisons actually being tested, then divide the overall significance level by that count. The family definition determines the adjustment.","The t calculation still uses each treatment-control mean difference and the shared ANOVA within-groups variance. Restricting the comparison family changes the number of tests, not the outcome measurements themselves."]},"9:13":{"title":"Two HDL comparisons against the inactive control","paragraphs":["The inactive group acts as the control. The selected comparisons are runners versus inactive men and joggers versus inactive men; runners versus joggers is not part of this family.","There are now two comparisons, so Bonferroni divides the overall significance level by two. This gives a less restrictive per-comparison threshold than dividing it among all three pairs.","Both selected differences exceed the adjusted critical magnitude. The conclusion addresses the two activity groups relative to the inactive control, without adding a result for the omitted pair."]},"9:14":{"title":"Holm for the two control comparisons","paragraphs":["Order the two treatment-control comparisons by strength of evidence. The larger runner-versus-inactive t magnitude is tested first.","Its initial threshold uses the overall level divided by two. Once it passes, the remaining jogger-versus-inactive comparison uses the full overall level for its Holm step.","Both comparisons reject their equal-means hypotheses. Keep the ordered decisions attached to their group labels so that the correct threshold and conclusion remain paired with each comparison."]},"9:15":{"title":"Matching R t-test functions to their questions","paragraphs":["The independent-samples function compares two groups of different individuals. The one-sample function compares one group's mean with a specified reference value.","The post-hoc function uses an existing ANOVA result for pairwise comparisons. Its role includes carrying the shared background variance into the follow-up analysis.","The generic t.test function can represent different tests depending on its arguments. Interpret the design and output rather than assuming the function name alone tells you whether the data were paired, independent, or compared with a reference."]},"9:16":{"title":"Work the five-comparison Holm exercise in order","paragraphs":["Sort the five t magnitudes from largest to smallest while preserving their comparison labels. The supplied residual degrees of freedom determine the t reference for every step.","Begin with the overall level divided by five, then use successive Holm levels only while earlier tests pass. A comparison that fails stops further rejection in the sequence.","The largest two magnitudes pass their respective thresholds, and the next does not. The remaining comparisons are therefore not rejected; they are not restarted later with more permissive thresholds."]},"10:1":{"title":"Sampling distributions and clinical-trial design","paragraphs":["The first part asks how statistics change across different samples. Expected values describe their centers, while estimator properties describe whether they are centered correctly and how much they fluctuate.","The central limit theorem explains the shape of many sample-mean distributions. The t and F distributions then provide reference patterns for other statistics constructed from sample data.","The clinical-trial part asks how the data were produced. A clear study question, comparison groups, random assignment, and masking determine what the observed statistical comparison can establish about treatment."]},"10:2":{"title":"Three uses of the t-test framework","paragraphs":["An independent-samples t test compares means from different groups of individuals. A one-sample test compares one mean with a fixed reference value.","Post-hoc pairwise testing follows an overall ANOVA and uses its within-groups error estimate. Bonferroni and Holm adjust the decisions when several pairs are tested.","The discussion connects each function to a different study question. Identify the number of groups, whether the benchmark was sampled, and whether the test belongs to a multiple-comparison family before interpreting its output."]},"10:3":{"title":"The method chooser connects design with data type","paragraphs":["The table organizes methods by the measurement scale and the experimental design. Numerical outcomes from two independent groups lead to an unpaired t test, while several independent groups lead to ANOVA.","Nominal category counts call for proportion or contingency-table methods instead of a comparison of numerical means. Other rows distinguish ordered data and survival outcomes.","Use the row and column together. Knowing only that there are two groups is insufficient; the outcome's meaning and whether the individuals are independent also affect the method."]},"10:4":{"title":"Why a sampling distribution is useful","paragraphs":["A sample describes the individuals actually observed. Inference asks what that sample reveals about the larger population from which it was selected.","Repeated samples would produce different statistics, even if the population stayed fixed. A sampling distribution describes that repeat-to-repeat variation.","Its width describes how much a statistic can fluctuate because of sampling. That supplies a reference for judging how precisely a single sample represents a population quantity."]},"10:5":{"title":"Expected value includes means and variances","paragraphs":["The mean averages possible values using density weights. A function of the variable can be averaged with the same weighting rule by changing what is multiplied by the density.","Squared distance from the population mean is one such function. Its expectation is the population variance, so variance itself is a particular expected value.","This gives a shared language for later derivations. For averages of values or squared distances, the density specifies which regions of the possible outcomes receive more weight."]},"10:6":{"title":"Linear combinations of independent variables","paragraphs":["A linear combination multiplies each variable by a constant and adds the results. Its expected value adds those same weighted means.","Its variance uses the squared constants. Independent sources of uncertainty contribute positively even if one of their coefficients is negative.","If the original variables are independent Normal variables, the combination is also Normal. These rules explain the center and spread of an average by treating every observation as one weighted contribution."]},"10:7":{"title":"Several ways to estimate mean birthweight","paragraphs":["The birthweight sample can be summarized by its mean, its median, or the average of its minimum and maximum. Each produces a candidate estimate of the population center.","Different samples cause each candidate to change. Comparing estimators therefore requires looking beyond one sample's numerical answer.","Ask where each estimator's repeated-sample distribution is centered and how wide it is. An estimate can look reasonable in one sample while varying much more than another method across repeated samples."]},"10:8":{"title":"Why the sample mean is useful here","paragraphs":["The expectation of an average equals the population mean, so repeated sample means are centered correctly. That property is called unbiasedness.","For independent observations, averaging reduces variance by the sample size. The standard error is the square root of that variance, describing the spread of repeated sample means.","For the Normal-population comparison shown, the sample mean has less variance than the displayed alternative center estimates. It combines correct centering with relatively stable repeated-sample behavior."]},"10:9":{"title":"A statistic becomes a new random variable","paragraphs":["Every new sample gives a new sample mean. Repeating that process produces a distribution of means, each based on the same specified sample size.","The same idea applies to t and F: both are calculated from sample data and would change if different observations were selected. They therefore have sampling distributions too.","A sampling distribution is about a statistic across possible samples. It is different from the distribution of individual observations inside one sample."]},"10:10":{"title":"Heights and averages of ten heights","paragraphs":["The original variable records an individual's height. The new variable records the average height of a sample of ten individuals.","Each average combines several original outcomes, so it has its own probability distribution. Its center is tied to the original population mean, while its spread is smaller than individual-height spread.","The plotted sample means illustrate only some possible averages. Repeating the sampling more extensively would build the fuller distribution used to describe uncertainty in that mean."]},"10:11":{"title":"What the central limit theorem changes","paragraphs":["For large independent samples, the sample mean is approximately Normal even when the original measurements are not. Its mean remains the population mean and its variance is reduced by sample size.","The approximation concerns the distribution of averages. It does not turn the original individual measurements into a Normal population.","If the population itself is Normal, the average is Normal at every sample size. That separate exact case explains why a small Normal sample can use a Normal mean distribution without relying on a large-sample approximation."]},"10:12":{"title":"Averages drawn from a Uniform population","paragraphs":["The original population is flat across its range, with a center of 100. Samples of five and fifteen produce distributions of averages that are more concentrated near that center.","The sample-mean shapes are no longer flat. Averaging makes central combinations more common than combinations containing mostly extreme values.","The standard error decreases as sample size grows. Compare the wider average distribution for five observations with the narrower one for fifteen while keeping their shared center in view."]},"10:13":{"title":"Larger averages from the Uniform population","paragraphs":["The samples of thirty and fifty give even narrower distributions of means. Their centers remain at the same population mean rather than drifting as sample size increases.","Their approximately bell-shaped appearance illustrates the central limit theorem. The original population was Uniform, yet its sufficiently large sample means resemble a Normal pattern.","The width follows the square-root sample-size rule. Increasing sample size improves precision, but doubling the sample size does not halve the standard error."]},"10:14":{"title":"Averages from a right-skewed population","paragraphs":["The original measurements have a long right tail and a mean of 100. Small samples can occasionally include a very large observation, pulling their mean upward.","Averages of five are still visibly skewed, while averages of fifteen are more concentrated and less asymmetric. Both remain centered around the same population mean across repeated sampling.","This example shows why starting shape matters for the speed of the Normal approximation. Averaging reduces scatter immediately, while a strongly skewed population may need larger samples for an approximately symmetric mean distribution."]},"10:15":{"title":"Larger samples reduce skewness in the mean distribution","paragraphs":["The averages of thirty and fifty from the skewed population are more bell shaped than the small-sample averages. A few extreme individuals have less influence when combined with more independent observations.","The standard errors are also smaller, so the means cluster more tightly around 100. Shape and precision are changing together, but they describe different features.","The population's individual distribution remains skewed. The central limit theorem explains the increasingly Normal-looking averages, not a change in the underlying individuals."]},"10:16":{"title":"Estimating spread changes Z into t","paragraphs":["When the population standard deviation is known, a standardized Normal sample mean can be compared with the standard Normal reference. Its denominator uses a fixed population spread.","In practice that spread is often unknown and replaced with the sample standard deviation. The denominator now fluctuates from sample to sample as well as the numerator.","The resulting statistic follows a t distribution for a Normal sample, with sample size minus one degrees of freedom. The extra denominator uncertainty is why it is not simply standard Normal."]},"10:17":{"title":"How degrees of freedom change the t curve","paragraphs":["The t curves are symmetric around zero but have heavier tails than the standard Normal curve when degrees of freedom are small. Extreme standardized results are more common because spread is estimated imprecisely.","As degrees of freedom increase, the estimate of spread becomes more stable. The t curve approaches the standard Normal curve.","The density expression defines the family mathematically, but table use depends on choosing the correct degrees of freedom. Different curves require different critical magnitudes for the same tail probability."]},"10:18":{"title":"F also has a family of sampling distributions","paragraphs":["F is constructed from sample data, so repeated samples produce different F values. Under equal population means, that variation has a reference density.","There are two degrees of freedom because the numerator and denominator each estimate variation. Both affect the curve's shape and its upper-tail critical value.","An F value therefore needs its design information before it can be interpreted. The same numerical ratio can correspond to different tail probabilities under different numerator and denominator degrees of freedom."]},"10:19":{"title":"Degrees of freedom and F-table cutoffs","paragraphs":["The illustrated table pattern shows critical F values changing as the reference distributions become less spread out. Larger denominator degrees of freedom make the background variance estimate more stable.","When chance is less likely to produce an extreme ratio, the upper-tail cutoff can move closer to one. This connects the shape of the reference curve with the table entries.","Always use the actual numerator and denominator degrees of freedom for the question. Read the relevant row and column rather than substituting a general impression about how critical values change."]},"10:20":{"title":"Lind's scurvy comparison","paragraphs":["Lind compared treatments among sailors with scurvy while keeping their general circumstances and diet similar. Separate patients received different additions to that shared care.","The comparison creates a way to relate outcomes to treatment conditions. Without a comparison, improvement after treatment could reflect other changes occurring at the same time.","The example introduces a central trial-design idea: define what differs between groups and what is held comparable. Statistical calculations become more informative when the data come from a clear treatment comparison."]},"10:21":{"title":"How controlled trials developed","paragraphs":["The historical table traces the development of controls, sham procedures, placebo treatment, and random allocation. These additions address different ways a treatment comparison can become misleading.","A control supplies a reference outcome, while random allocation determines who receives which treatment. Rules for human studies also address how participants are protected and studies are reviewed.","Read the timeline as a progression in study design rather than a list of dates to calculate with. The important connection is between each design feature and the source of bias or uncertainty it helps address."]},"10:22":{"title":"An example linking historical and modern medicine","paragraphs":["The article excerpt discusses interacting influences on disease and uses an estrogen-receptor example to connect historical ideas with modern biological mechanisms. Its theme is that outcomes can reflect several interacting processes.","A proposed biological explanation and a clinical treatment comparison answer different questions. Understanding a mechanism can motivate a study question, while patient data are still needed to evaluate treatment outcomes.","This page broadens the context for the trial discussion. Keep the claim being investigated separate from the design that would supply evidence for it."]},"10:23":{"title":"State the primary trial question in advance","paragraphs":["A trial begins with a clearly defined primary question and a protocol describing how it will be investigated. The question determines the population, intervention, comparison, and outcome.","The example asks whether isoniazid prevents tuberculosis disease or infection in a specified infant population. The title also identifies randomization, masking, and a placebo comparison.","Those details define what is actually being tested. A clear advance question keeps the eventual statistical analysis tied to a specified treatment claim rather than whichever result looks most favorable afterward."]},"10:24":{"title":"Following participants through a randomized trial","paragraphs":["The diagram begins with a population affected by the condition. Eligibility and consent determine who enters the study before random assignment divides participants between treatments A and B.","Randomization occurs after those entry steps. It aims to make the treatment groups comparable by assigning treatment independently of participant characteristics.","The final comparison is between outcomes under A and B. Distinguish random assignment within the study from how the study participants were selected from the wider patient population."]},"10:25":{"title":"Randomization and whom the trial represents","paragraphs":["Random assignment tends to balance participant characteristics across treatment groups. That supports the statistical comparison by reducing systematic differences in who receives each treatment.","The volunteers who enter a trial may still differ from the general patient population. Group comparability inside the study and applicability outside it are therefore separate questions.","Acceptability also affects enrollment: patients or physicians may decline randomization, and some treatment assignments raise ethical issues. These design considerations affect how a randomized study can actually be conducted."]},"10:26":{"title":"A crossover trial uses both treatments within people","paragraphs":["One sequence receives A then B, and the other receives B then A. Each participant experiences both treatments in different periods.","Comparing a person with themselves can reduce variation from stable individual differences. This can make a crossover design more efficient than comparing entirely separate sets of people.","The disease must be sufficiently stable, and one period's treatment must not carry into the next comparison. Otherwise a later outcome mixes the current treatment with previous treatment or changing disease status."]},"10:27":{"title":"Masking reduces expectation-related bias","paragraphs":["Masking hides treatment identity from people involved in the trial. Participants, clinical teams, evaluators, and statistical teams can each influence a different part of the study.","A participant's expectation may affect reported symptoms, while an evaluator's expectation may affect a subjective assessment. Hiding assignments reduces those pathways for treatment knowledge to influence outcomes.","Masking is particularly useful when measurement or treatment involves judgment. Its purpose is distinct from randomization: assignment balances groups, while masking reduces bias after assignments are made."]},"10:28":{"title":"Single, double, and triple masking","paragraphs":["In an open trial, patients know the treatment they receive. In a single-blind trial, the patient does not know the assignment.","Double blinding also hides assignment from the healthcare provider. Triple blinding extends masking to the statistical or monitoring role specified here.","The labels describe who is kept unaware, so identify the people involved rather than memorizing the number alone. The practical aim is to reduce the influence of treatment expectations on care, reporting, evaluation, and analysis."]},"10:29":{"title":"Enough participants, recruited and retained","paragraphs":["Sample size must be large enough for a clinically meaningful difference to be detectable with the planned analysis. Too little information can leave an important effect hard to distinguish from sampling variation.","Recruitment concerns obtaining the needed participants within a feasible time. A plan on paper does not produce a useful sample unless eligible people actually enroll.","Adherence concerns whether participants follow the assigned treatment. Monitoring and supporting it helps preserve the intended comparison between treatments as the study proceeds."]},"10:30":{"title":"Improvement after surgery needs a comparison","paragraphs":["The original artery-ligation report followed treated patients and recorded symptom changes. Improvement after an intervention alone does not isolate what caused that improvement.","The later double-blind randomized comparison included a control procedure. It did not find the reported objective difference in stress testing or ECG between groups.","The example shows why treatment experience and expectations belong in the comparison design. A controlled trial can separate the procedure's specific effect from changes that also occur under a sham intervention."]},"10:31":{"title":"The sham-controlled ORBITA example","paragraphs":["ORBITA compared coronary intervention with a placebo procedure in patients with angina after medical treatment was optimized. The study used randomization and masking.","Exercise time was assessed before and after the procedure, allowing comparison of the change between the assigned groups. Both groups experienced the procedure setting, while the active intervention differed.","The excerpt reports no improvement in exercise time beyond the placebo-procedure effect for this comparison. The key teaching point is how a sham control helps evaluate a procedure's specific contribution to the measured outcome."]},"10:32":{"title":"Where patients receive care affects the sample","paragraphs":["The stacked diagram follows a broad community population through reporting illness, seeking care, visiting different services, and hospitalization. Only a small subset reaches an academic medical center.","Each step selects people from the previous group. Patients observed in a specialized hospital may therefore have different characteristics from everyone experiencing illness in the community.","A study's recruitment setting affects whom its results represent. Connect the final clinical sample back to the larger population before assuming that findings transfer unchanged to every patient."]},"11:1":{"title":"Comparing proportions instead of means","paragraphs":["A nominal outcome places each person in a category, such as lived or died. Count the people in each category and divide by the relevant group total to obtain proportions.","A comparison of two proportions asks whether the population chances of the specified outcome are equal. The z statistic scales the observed proportion difference by its sampling uncertainty.","The lecture then introduces Yates's continuity correction for the course's two-by-two comparisons. The anesthetic and aspirin examples show how the same categorical framework applies to different clinical outcomes."]},"11:2":{"title":"Find the nominal-data branch of the chooser","paragraphs":["The highlighted nominal-data branch applies when the response is category membership rather than a measured numerical quantity. With two independent groups, a proportion test or two-by-two contingency analysis can address a binary outcome.","The numerical-data branch still contains the unpaired t test and ANOVA. Those compare means of measurements, so they answer a different kind of question.","Begin with what was recorded for each subject. A zero-or-one code for an outcome does not turn that clinical category into an ordinary continuous measurement."]},"11:3":{"title":"Counts become group-specific outcome rates","paragraphs":["The anesthetic table separates lived and died outcomes for halothane and morphine. Each row's total is the denominator for that treatment group's death proportion.","The numbers of deaths cannot be compared fairly without those totals because the groups contain different numbers of subjects. A proportion expresses deaths relative to how many people were at risk in that group.","The question is whether the population death probabilities differ between anesthetic groups. Nominal outcomes support a count-and-proportion analysis, while an ordered pain score would carry an additional ranking structure."]},"11:4":{"title":"Two categories cover the population","paragraphs":["Fifty of the two hundred individuals are left-footed, with the remainder right-footed. These categories are mutually exclusive: one individual belongs to only one of the two groups in this model.","They are also exhaustive, so every individual is counted somewhere. The proportion in one category determines the other as the remainder from one.","The population proportion is the number with the attribute divided by the full population size. It describes how likely a randomly selected individual is to have that attribute."]},"11:5":{"title":"A zero-one mean is a proportion","paragraphs":["Code the attribute as one when present and zero when absent. Adding those codes simply counts how many individuals have the attribute, because only the ones contribute.","Dividing that sum by the population size therefore produces the population proportion. The mean of this binary random variable is the probability of the coded-one outcome.","Attach the outcome label to the code. A mean proportion of deaths and a mean proportion of survivors refer to opposite events even though the mathematical structure is the same."]},"11:6":{"title":"Variance of a binary outcome","paragraphs":["For a zero-one variable, squaring an outcome leaves it unchanged. Both its ordinary mean and its expected squared value therefore equal the probability of one.","Subtracting the squared mean from the expected squared value gives binary population variance. It depends on both the coded-one probability and its complement.","This is variation among individual binary outcomes. It is not yet the smaller uncertainty in a sample proportion, which will also depend on how many individuals are sampled."]},"11:7":{"title":"Deriving binary mean and variance from probabilities","paragraphs":["The probability function gives zero a weight of one minus the event probability and one a weight equal to the event probability. Their weighted average is the event probability itself.","For variance, measure each outcome's distance from that mean, square it, and apply the same two weights. Both possible outcomes contribute to the spread.","The simplified result is the product of the event probability and its complement. This connects the binary formula directly to the general expected-squared-distance definition already used for other random variables."]},"11:8":{"title":"Binary spread is greatest near an even split","paragraphs":["When almost everyone is in the same category, the binary values barely vary. At a probability of zero or one, all individuals have the same code and the spread is zero.","Near an even split, both zero and one occur frequently. That produces the largest individual spread for this coding.","The plotted curve shows standard deviation as the square root of binary variance. Its maximum occurs at a population proportion of one half, rather than increasing continuously as the proportion moves toward one."]},"11:9":{"title":"A sample proportion fluctuates with selection","paragraphs":["The full population contains a fixed left-footed proportion. A sample of ten contains only the individuals actually selected, so its category count may not match that population proportion exactly.","Code the sampled attribute as one or zero and count the ones. Dividing by ten gives this sample's estimate of the population probability.","Another random sample can give a different count and proportion. This sampling fluctuation is why estimating a proportion also requires describing the uncertainty of that estimate."]},"11:10":{"title":"Estimate a population probability with the sample count","paragraphs":["The sample contains a specified number of coded-one outcomes among all sampled subjects. Their ratio is the sample proportion, often read as p-hat.","Use that estimate in place of the unknown population probability to estimate individual binary variance. The product with its complement describes variation of zero-one outcomes under the fitted probability model.","Keep count, sample size, and proportion separate. The count changes with how many people are observed, while the proportion expresses the count relative to its denominator."]},"11:11":{"title":"Standard error of an estimated proportion","paragraphs":["The sample proportion is the average of the zero-one observations. Its sampling variance is therefore the individual binary variance divided by sample size.","Taking the square root gives the standard error of the proportion. This describes how much estimated proportions would vary across repeated samples of the same size.","A larger sample reduces that uncertainty without necessarily changing the underlying event probability. Distinguish individual binary spread from the precision of its estimated population proportion."]},"11:12":{"title":"Death proportions in the anesthetic samples","paragraphs":["For halothane, count eight deaths among sixty-one subjects; for morphine, count ten among sixty-seven. Each ratio estimates its group's population death probability.","The estimated standard errors are similar because the proportions and sample sizes are fairly close. They describe precision of the estimated death rates, not the variability of an individual's survival status.","For the binary spread calculation, multiply each proportion by its own complement. The original halothane variance and standard-deviation line contains an arithmetic error; the displayed death count and proportion should remain the starting point for the calculation."]},"11:13":{"title":"Standardize the difference of proportions","paragraphs":["Subtract the two sample proportions to describe the observed group gap. The denominator measures how much such a difference can fluctuate because of sampling.","The initial form combines the two groups' separate variance contributions. Both sample sizes matter because both estimated proportions are uncertain.","An approximately standard Normal reference converts the standardized gap into a test decision. For a two-sided comparison, a large magnitude in either direction supplies evidence against equal population proportions."]},"11:14":{"title":"Pool the event probability under equality","paragraphs":["Under the null hypothesis, the two groups share one population event probability. Estimate it by combining their event counts and dividing by their combined sample size.","Use that pooled probability and its complement in the standard error of the difference. The reciprocal sample sizes still account for how much information each group supplies.","Pooling applies to the null-model uncertainty, while the numerator retains the observed difference between the two sample proportions. The two roles should not be interchanged."]},"11:15":{"title":"Yates correction for the two-by-two comparison","paragraphs":["The observed outcome counts are discrete, while the z reference is continuous. The course applies Yates's correction to its two-by-two comparisons to account for that difference.","The correction reduces the absolute observed proportion gap before it is divided by its pooled standard error. Keep it in the numerator rather than changing the group counts or the pooled probability.","Compare the resulting magnitude with a two-sided Normal cutoff. The infinite-degrees-of-freedom row of the supplied two-tailed t table gives those same Normal critical magnitudes."]},"11:16":{"title":"State the anesthetic proportion hypothesis","paragraphs":["The halothane and morphine rows record two outcomes, lived or died. This is a two-group comparison of a binary event.","Define the event as death and state that its population proportion is equal in the two anesthetic groups under the null. The alternative allows either treatment group to have a different rate.","Use each row total when estimating its death proportion. Then combine the death counts and group totals for the pooled null estimate used in the corrected test."]},"11:17":{"title":"The corrected anesthetic z is near zero","paragraphs":["The estimated death proportions are close, and the continuity correction further reduces their observed absolute gap. The displayed corrected statistic has a very small magnitude.","Such a value lies near the center of the null reference distribution rather than in an extreme tail. It does not meet the rejection criterion.","Do not reject equal population death proportions for these data. Keep this conclusion separate from the earlier cardiac-index comparison: the groups are similar, but the outcome and statistical method differ."]},"11:18":{"title":"Use the Normal row for the proportion z cutoff","paragraphs":["The highlighted bottom row is the limiting t distribution at infinitely many degrees of freedom, which matches the standard Normal reference. It provides critical magnitudes for the z test.","Choose the column for the intended two-tailed significance level. The 5% column gives the familiar critical magnitude near 1.96.","The categorical z statistic does not use the small-sample t degrees of freedom from the two-sample mean test. This table row is being used as a convenient source of Normal cutoffs."]},"11:19":{"title":"Aspirin and placebo: define the thrombus event","paragraphs":["The table records whether dialysis patients developed thrombi. There are separate totals for the placebo and aspirin groups.","The placebo group has eighteen events among twenty-five patients, while the aspirin group has six among nineteen. Compare rates within their own denominators rather than only comparing the event counts.","The null states that the two population thrombus probabilities are equal. The observed lower aspirin rate is the difference whose sampling uncertainty the corrected z test will evaluate."]},"11:20":{"title":"Calculate the corrected aspirin-versus-placebo z","paragraphs":["Calculate each event proportion, then pool all thrombus events across the combined group size. The pooled event probability is a little above one half.","Subtract the continuity correction from the absolute difference of the two sample proportions. Divide that corrected gap by the standard error built from the pooled probability and both group sizes.","The result is a z magnitude around 2.36. Carry the proportions without early rounding; the original display abbreviates the pooled value, but the pooled count ratio supplies the more accurate calculation."]},"11:21":{"title":"Interpret the aspirin proportion-test result","paragraphs":["The corrected z magnitude exceeds the 5% two-sided Normal cutoff. It also exceeds the displayed cutoff for a total two-tailed level of 0.02.","Reject equal population thrombus probabilities. The sample event rate is lower in the aspirin group, which gives the direction of the observed difference.","The statistic establishes evidence of a difference, while the group proportions describe its size. Later relative-risk and absolute-risk calculations express that size in clinically interpretable terms."]},"11:22":{"title":"Adverse reactions to anesthetic gel","paragraphs":["The exercise compares topical anesthetic gel with placebo cream during arterial blood sampling. The recorded outcome is whether an adverse reaction occurred within the observation period.","Three of thirty-six gel recipients and eight of forty placebo recipients had a reaction. Each subject contributes one binary outcome, making this a two-by-two proportion comparison.","State the equal-population-reaction-rate null, calculate the two proportions and pooled event rate, and apply the course's continuity correction. Use the resulting statistic to decide whether the observed rate difference exceeds sampling variation at the chosen level."]},"12:1":{"title":"From two proportions to contingency tables","paragraphs":["A contingency table organizes counts by group and outcome. Under a no-association null, the outcome distribution should be the same across groups.","Expected counts show what that shared distribution predicts for each group size. The chi-squared statistic measures the mismatch between observed and expected counts.","The lecture extends from two-by-two tables to larger tables and adjusted pairwise follow-ups. Goodness of fit then compares one observed category distribution with a specified set of expected probabilities."]},"12:2":{"title":"Fall-recess discussion notice","paragraphs":["The notice states that there is no discussion meeting during fall recess. It does not introduce a new probability calculation or statistical method.","The lecture collection still contains the contingency-table material. The absence of a discussion meeting does not change the definitions of observed counts, expected counts, or the comparison being developed.","Use the lecture examples as the material for this part of the sequence. The mathematical teaching resumes with the method chooser and the categorical-data comparison on the following pages."]},"12:3":{"title":"Categorical group comparisons in the chooser","paragraphs":["The nominal row contains tests based on category counts. Two groups with binary outcomes can be compared using a z proportion test or a two-by-two chi-squared analysis.","Several groups require a larger contingency-table comparison. The numerical-outcome row instead leads to t tests and ANOVA, which compare measurement means.","The highlighted cells connect the new method to the study design. Decide what the outcome records and how many independent groups are present before selecting the reference statistic."]},"12:4":{"title":"The aspirin z test as a bridge","paragraphs":["The aspirin table contains the same counts used for the corrected z calculation. Its observed proportion difference is standardized using a pooled null estimate.","The resulting magnitude is about 2.36, exceeding the two-sided Normal boundary used for rejection. The group rates supply the direction of the observed difference.","The next method analyzes these same counts by comparing each table cell with its expected count under no treatment effect. The data and null question remain the same while the statistic is expressed differently."]},"12:5":{"title":"What no treatment effect predicts for the table","paragraphs":["The observed table separates placebo and aspirin patients into thrombus and no-thrombus outcomes. The row totals fix how many patients belong to each treatment group.","Under no treatment effect, both rows should share the same population outcome probabilities. Estimate that common outcome distribution from the column totals across both groups.","Expected counts answer how those outcomes would be distributed across the fixed row sizes under that null. They are predictions from the model, not replacement observations."]},"12:6":{"title":"Pool the outcome distribution before allocating it","paragraphs":["Across both treatment groups, twenty-four of forty-four patients developed thrombi. The remaining twenty were free of thrombi.","Under the no-effect null, apply those pooled outcome proportions to both treatment groups. A larger treatment group then receives more expected events simply because it contains more patients.","This separates the null prediction from the observed treatment difference. The pooled distribution supplies the shared rates; the row totals determine how many expected outcomes each group receives."]},"12:7":{"title":"Expected counts preserve both sets of totals","paragraphs":["Multiply each group size by the pooled thrombus proportion to obtain its expected event count. Use the pooled no-thrombus proportion for the other cell in the same row.","The expected placebo events are higher than the expected aspirin events because the placebo group is larger. These are counts, not different null event probabilities.","The resulting table retains the observed row and column totals. Fractional expected counts are acceptable because they represent model averages rather than actual fractions of a patient."]},"12:8":{"title":"Match each observation with its expected cell","paragraphs":["The arrows pair the placebo-thrombus count with its own expected count, and do the same for the other three cells. The comparison must stay within corresponding group-outcome positions.","The observed placebo row has more thrombi than expected under no effect, while the aspirin row has fewer. The no-thrombus cells balance those discrepancies because the margins are fixed.","Each observed-minus-expected difference will contribute to one overall statistic. Keeping the cell labels attached prevents an event count from being compared with the wrong row or outcome prediction."]},"12:9":{"title":"Chi-squared adds standardized cell discrepancies","paragraphs":["For each cell, compare its observed count with its expected count. Squaring the discrepancy makes both excesses and shortages contribute positively.","Dividing by the expected count expresses the mismatch relative to its expected size. Add the cell contributions to obtain the chi-squared statistic.","For the two-by-two case, the course uses Yates's correction before squaring the absolute discrepancy. There is one degree of freedom because the fixed margins leave only one independent cell count."]},"12:10":{"title":"Read the chi-squared upper-tail table","paragraphs":["The chi-squared reference uses nonnegative values, with large values representing large overall mismatch from the null prediction. The rejection region lies in its upper tail.","Choose the row for the table's degrees of freedom and the column for the desired upper-tail probability. Their intersection gives the critical statistic.","Reject the null when the observed statistic exceeds that critical value. Do not treat the calculated statistic itself as a probability; the table supplies its reference-tail interpretation."]},"12:11":{"title":"The corrected aspirin chi-squared calculation","paragraphs":["Each of the four aspirin-placebo cells contributes its corrected squared discrepancy divided by its expected count. Adding those contributions gives a statistic near 5.58.","The two-by-two table has one degree of freedom. Its statistic exceeds the relevant rejection boundary, supporting a treatment-outcome difference.","This agrees with the earlier corrected z result for the same table. Squaring that z magnitude gives the corresponding one-degree-of-freedom chi-squared value when the same correction and precision are used."]},"12:12":{"title":"Extend expected counts to any table size","paragraphs":["For each cell, multiply its row total by its column total and divide by the grand total. This assigns the null outcome proportions consistently across all groups.","Add the ordinary squared observed-minus-expected contributions over every row and column. The course reserves Yates's correction for the two-by-two case.","Degrees of freedom equal rows minus one times columns minus one. Both dimensions matter because fixed row and column totals constrain how many cell values can vary independently."]},"12:13":{"title":"Three depression-treatment groups","paragraphs":["The rows represent nefazodone, psychotherapy, and their combination. The columns contain the two outcome categories named in the supplied table.","The group sizes differ, so raw event counts alone do not give a fair rate comparison. Each outcome count belongs with its row total.","The null says the population outcome distribution is the same across the three treatments. Keep the source table's outcome labels attached when interpreting the direction, rather than assuming what a larger entry means clinically."]},"12:14":{"title":"Build the depression example's expected table","paragraphs":["The grand total combines all three treatment groups. The column proportions provide the shared outcome probabilities under the no-treatment-difference null.","Apply those proportions to each group's row total. The expected counts vary by row size, while each row uses the same null outcome probabilities.","Compare the observed table with the expected table cell by cell. The combined-treatment row has a particularly large discrepancy in the first outcome column, contributing strongly to the overall test statistic."]},"12:15":{"title":"Test the three-by-two depression table","paragraphs":["The ordinary chi-squared calculation sums six cell contributions. This is a three-by-two table, so it uses two degrees of freedom and no two-by-two continuity correction.","The statistic is approximately 21.2. It exceeds the displayed upper-tail critical value even at the stringent significance level of 0.001.","Reject equal population outcome distributions across all three treatments. The overall result establishes a difference somewhere, motivating follow-up comparisons to identify which treatment pairs account for it."]},"12:16":{"title":"Nefazodone versus psychotherapy as a new subtable","paragraphs":["Keep only the nefazodone and psychotherapy rows. Their outcome counts form a separate two-by-two table for this pairwise question.","Recalculate the column totals and grand total using these two rows only. The combined-treatment patients do not belong in this pair's expected-count calculation.","Use the new margins to fill the expected table, then apply Yates's correction. A subtable has its own null prediction even though it originated inside a larger analysis."]},"12:17":{"title":"Nefazodone versus combined treatment","paragraphs":["Select the nefazodone and combined-treatment rows. The first outcome column now has a different pooled total from the previous pairwise comparison.","Calculate new expected counts from this subtable's row totals, column totals, and grand total. Reusing the larger table's expected cells would not represent this pair's null model.","This two-by-two statistic tests equality of outcome proportions for these two treatments specifically. It will later be interpreted within the family of three pairwise comparisons."]},"12:18":{"title":"Psychotherapy versus combined treatment","paragraphs":["The selected rows now compare psychotherapy with the combination. Their group sizes remain attached to the corresponding observed outcome counts.","The two retained rows define a new pooled outcome distribution. Use those new margins to calculate the four expected counts.","After applying the two-by-two continuity correction, this statistic joins the other two pairwise results. Keep its treatment label attached so it receives the correct place in the later ordered adjustment."]},"12:19":{"title":"Calculate all three corrected pairwise statistics","paragraphs":["Every selected pair is a two-by-two table, so each uses Yates's correction and one degree of freedom. The original overall table used a different dimension and calculation.","The nefazodone-versus-psychotherapy discrepancy is small. The two comparisons involving combined treatment produce much larger chi-squared values.","These statistics rank the strength of the pairwise evidence, but multiple testing still requires an adjustment. Do not declare each pair significant using three unrelated unadjusted decisions."]},"12:20":{"title":"Holm thresholds for the three chi-squared comparisons","paragraphs":["Order the chi-squared statistics from largest to smallest because all three use the same one-degree-of-freedom reference. The strongest observed discrepancy is tested first.","The overall significance level is divided among the remaining hypotheses at each Holm step. Start with three, then two, then one if earlier comparisons pass.","Use the appropriate upper-tail chi-squared critical value at every step. The first displayed lookup uses an available stricter table column to establish rejection when the exact requested column is absent."]},"12:21":{"title":"Interpret the adjusted depression comparisons","paragraphs":["The two larger statistics pass their successive Holm thresholds. Their comparisons both involve the combined-treatment group.","The small nefazodone-versus-psychotherapy statistic does not pass its threshold. The familywise conclusion therefore identifies differences for the two combination comparisons without rejecting equality for that remaining pair.","State the treatment pairs explicitly and retain the table's outcome definition. Statistical evidence of a different response distribution is the result; deciding which direction is clinically favorable requires reading the outcome label correctly."]},"12:22":{"title":"Goodness of fit starts with one category distribution","paragraphs":["A goodness-of-fit test uses one set of observed category counts. The null supplies the expected proportion for each category, often from a previous study or specified hypothesis.","Multiply each expected probability by the current total sample size to obtain expected counts. Compare observed and expected counts using their squared discrepancies divided by expected counts.","With specified category probabilities, the degrees of freedom are the number of categories minus one. This differs from the row-by-column degrees of freedom used for an independence table."]},"12:23":{"title":"Exercise behavior before and after a campaign","paragraphs":["The earlier survey supplies three expected proportions: no regular exercise, sporadic exercise, and regular exercise. The later survey supplies observed counts from a new graduating class.","The question asks whether the later category distribution matches the earlier pattern. There is one current sample being compared with specified benchmark probabilities.","Use the later total of 470 students to scale those earlier probabilities into expected counts. Do not compare a current raw count directly with an earlier percentage."]},"12:24":{"title":"Complete the exercise-behavior goodness-of-fit test","paragraphs":["The expected counts are 282, 117.5, and 70.5. Compare each with its matching observed count and add the three standardized squared discrepancies.","The statistic is about 8.457, with two degrees of freedom. It exceeds the displayed critical value for an upper-tail probability of 0.025.","Reject the hypothesis that the current category proportions match the earlier distribution. The conclusion concerns a changed exercise-behavior pattern; the test is based on the whole set of category differences."]},"12:25":{"title":"Place the completed categorical methods on the map","paragraphs":["The marked nominal-data cells now include two-group proportion tests and larger contingency-table comparisons. Both work with category counts rather than means of continuous measurements.","The numerical-data row remains the route to t tests and ANOVA. The method depends jointly on response type and group structure.","Use this map to organize the analyses already learned. First identify the data being recorded, then the independent groups being compared, and finally the corresponding test statistic."]},"12:26":{"title":"Association adds a question about effect size","paragraphs":["The highlighted association column moves beyond testing whether category distributions differ. Relative risk and odds ratios quantify the direction and size of an association between nominal variables.","A significant chi-squared result alone does not say how many times more likely an event is. Its statistic measures mismatch with independence, rather than directly expressing a risk ratio.","The next methods attach interpretable measures to the observed event pattern. Their choice depends on the study design and on which event probabilities can be estimated from the data."]},"12:27":{"title":"Keep the study design attached to the association measure","paragraphs":["The same method map links independent-group category tests with relative risk and odds ratios. Testing and quantifying an association answer related but distinct questions.","A test evaluates the equal-proportion or independence null. An association measure describes how event occurrence differs between the specified groups.","Before calculating the measure, identify whether the study followed exposed groups forward or selected people by their outcome. That distinction determines whether the table supports direct event-risk estimates or an odds-based comparison."]},"13:1":{"title":"Describe how large an association is","paragraphs":["A categorical test asks whether the observed pattern is compatible with no association. Relative risk and odds ratios instead describe the size and direction of the association.","The study design determines which quantities can be estimated. Following treatment or exposure groups forward provides event risks, while selecting cases and controls leads naturally to an odds comparison.","NNT and NNH express absolute outcome differences as numbers of patients. They connect a rate change with how many people must receive treatment for an additional benefit or harm."]},"13:2":{"title":"From the nominal test branch to association measures","paragraphs":["The chooser places relative risk and odds ratios in the association column for nominal variables. Their inputs are category counts, like the contingency tests already studied.","The purpose has changed from obtaining a rejection decision to quantifying a group relationship. A large sample can make a small association statistically significant, so the association's size deserves its own description.","Read the event and comparison direction before calculating. A treatment-to-control ratio and its reciprocal describe opposite reference choices even when they use the same table."]},"13:3":{"title":"Follow-up studies versus case-control sampling","paragraphs":["A randomized clinical trial assigns treatment and then observes outcomes. A prospective cohort study also follows people forward, but exposure is not randomly assigned.","A retrospective case-control study begins by selecting people with and without the outcome. Researchers then investigate their earlier exposure histories.","These designs build their samples in different directions. Forward follow-up can estimate outcome probabilities within exposure groups; sampling by outcome does not directly reproduce those population event risks."]},"13:4":{"title":"Association means the quantities vary together","paragraphs":["Two measured quantities are associated when knowing one changes expectations about the other. Their patterns are statistically dependent.","An association by itself does not establish a causal direction. A relationship may reflect other factors as well as a direct effect.","For nominal variables, the question becomes how category membership changes the observed outcome pattern. Relative risk and odds ratios put numbers on that relationship using explicitly defined groups and events."]},"13:5":{"title":"Relative risk compares two event probabilities","paragraphs":["Calculate the event probability within the treatment group and within the control group. Relative risk divides the treatment probability by the control probability.","A ratio below one means the event is less common in the treatment group; a ratio above one means it is more common. One corresponds to equal event risks.","The measure requires meaningful event-risk denominators, as supplied by a clinical trial or prospective cohort. Keep the event definition fixed in both groups before forming the ratio."]},"13:6":{"title":"Aspirin: relative and absolute reductions","paragraphs":["The event is thrombus formation. Its observed risk is six out of nineteen with aspirin and eighteen out of twenty-five with placebo.","Dividing the aspirin risk by the placebo risk gives a relative risk near 0.44. Subtracting the aspirin risk from the placebo risk gives an absolute reduction near 0.40.","These describe the same data on different scales. The ratio compares how large one risk is relative to the other; the difference describes how many fewer events occur per patient treated."]},"13:7":{"title":"Interpret the aspirin effect in words","paragraphs":["A relative risk around 0.44 means the aspirin group's thrombus risk is about forty-four hundredths of the placebo group's risk. The placebo group is the denominator reference.","The absolute reduction is about forty percentage points. It describes a difference between probabilities, rather than saying that every patient's risk falls by the same number of percentage points.","Name the event when stating either result. A smaller risk is favorable here because the event is thrombus formation; a beneficial event would require a different interpretation of direction."]},"13:8":{"title":"NNT converts an absolute benefit into patients","paragraphs":["Number needed to treat describes how many patients receive the treatment for one additional favorable outcome compared with the reference condition. It is based on the absolute outcome difference.","A larger absolute benefit means fewer people need treatment for one additional benefit. A small absolute benefit produces a larger NNT.","Use probabilities as decimals when taking the reciprocal. A percentage-point difference must first be converted to a proportion, and a request for a whole number of patients calls for rounding upward."]},"13:9":{"title":"NNT for preventing a thrombus","paragraphs":["The aspirin-placebo absolute risk reduction is a little above 0.40. Its reciprocal is approximately 2.5 patients per additional avoided thrombus.","That is an average comparative benefit, rather than a promise about a particular small group of patients. It comes from the difference in event rates between the conditions.","If a whole-patient answer is requested, round upward to three. Keep the original unrounded risk difference through the reciprocal calculation before performing that final rounding."]},"13:10":{"title":"NNH converts an absolute harm into patients","paragraphs":["Number needed to harm uses the absolute increase in an adverse outcome associated with treatment or exposure. Its reciprocal expresses one additional harmful outcome per number exposed.","A smaller NNH means harm occurs more frequently in the comparison. A larger NNH means more people must be exposed for one additional harm on average.","Define the adverse event and reference group explicitly. The arithmetic resembles NNT, but the direction and clinical meaning depend on whether the outcome difference represents benefit or harm."]},"13:11":{"title":"A large relative reduction can have a small absolute benefit","paragraphs":["The hypothetical disease causes ten deaths per thousand patients without the drug and eight per thousand with it. The relative reduction is twenty percent, but the absolute reduction is only two per thousand.","That small absolute benefit gives an NNT of five hundred. The baseline risk matters because the same relative reduction can correspond to very different numbers of prevented events.","If serious harm occurs once per twenty treated patients, treating five hundred leads to many harms per additional prevented death. Compare benefit and harm on the same patient scale and over the stated outcome period."]},"13:12":{"title":"Why case-control data lead to odds","paragraphs":["A retrospective study selects people who already have the disease and a comparison group without it. Exposure histories are then assessed within those outcome groups.","The numbers of cases and controls are chosen by the study, so their combined table does not directly reproduce the source population's disease rate. Direct disease risk by exposure cannot be read from those sampled totals.","The odds of exposure within cases and within controls can still be compared. Matching controls and choosing them independently of exposure help make that comparison meaningful."]},"13:13":{"title":"Constructing an odds ratio from a two-by-two table","paragraphs":["Within cases, divide exposed people by unexposed people to get exposure odds. Do the same within controls, then divide the two odds.","The resulting cross-product ratio compares exposure odds between outcome groups. An odds ratio of one indicates equal odds; values above or below one indicate the direction of association.","For prospective data, the same cross product can also compare disease odds between exposure groups. Keep odds separate from risk: odds use events relative to nonevents, while risk uses events relative to everyone at risk."]},"13:14":{"title":"Secondhand smoke and breast-cancer odds","paragraphs":["The case group contains fifty exposed and fourteen unexposed women. The control group contains forty-three exposed and thirty-five unexposed women.","Compare exposure odds in cases with exposure odds in controls. The cross-product calculation gives an odds ratio near 2.91.","Interpret it as higher odds of secondhand-smoke exposure among the cases relative to controls. Do not replace the word odds with risk, because this case-control sampling did not directly estimate population disease probabilities."]},"13:15":{"title":"Oral contraceptives and myocardial infarction","paragraphs":["The table follows oral-contraceptive users and nonusers for myocardial infarction over three years. Each exposure group has an event count and a much larger nonevent count.","The denominators describe the people followed in each group, so event risks can be calculated for this prospective example. The same table also supports an odds ratio.","First identify which row is exposed and which column is the event. This fixes the reference direction before comparing users with nonusers."]},"13:16":{"title":"Interpret the oral-contraceptive odds ratio","paragraphs":["The cross product gives an odds ratio near 3.72 for users relative to nonusers. It can be expressed as disease odds by exposure or exposure odds by disease for this table.","The two readings use the same algebraic cross product. Their words must still match the numerator and denominator groups being described.","This is an odds comparison, even though the event is rare and an odds ratio may resemble a risk ratio numerically. Preserve the measure's name and the three-year outcome period in the interpretation."]},"13:17":{"title":"Hormone therapy and survival in a cohort","paragraphs":["The example follows women grouped by hormone-replacement-therapy use and records survival outcomes. The table separates deaths and survivors for current users and women who never used therapy.","First test whether the outcome proportions differ using the appropriate two-by-two method. Then quantify the association with event risks because the cohort design provides exposed-group denominators.","Define death as the event and current use relative to never use as the comparison. That makes a relative risk below one mean a lower observed death risk among current users in this study."]},"13:18":{"title":"Smoking and renal-cell cancer in a case-control study","paragraphs":["The researchers recruited cancer cases and matched controls without renal-cell cancer, then asked about prior cigarette smoking. Selection began with the outcome rather than following smokers forward.","The table separates ever-smokers and never-smokers within cases and controls. A categorical test evaluates whether exposure and case status are associated.","Quantify that association with an odds ratio using the cross product. The case-control sampling supports an exposure-odds comparison, rather than a direct estimate of population cancer risk among smokers."]},"13:19":{"title":"R functions for categorical comparisons","paragraphs":["The chi-squared function analyzes a supplied contingency table. Table-building functions first organize individual records into the appropriate category counts.","The goodness-of-fit function instead compares one observed category vector with specified expected probabilities. Equal probabilities are one possible null, but the course examples also use unequal benchmark probabilities.","Read the function's inputs to identify the question: association across rows and columns, or fit of one category distribution to a target. The statistical interpretation comes from that setup rather than the command name alone."]},"15:1":{"title":"The midterm connects probability with inference","paragraphs":["The review begins with describing and classifying data, then moves to random variables and probability. These supply the language for centers, spread, and uncertain outcomes.","Sampling distributions explain how sample statistics fluctuate. ANOVA, t, z, and chi-squared tests use those reference patterns to evaluate population hypotheses.","Association measures describe the size of categorical relationships, while the R section checks interpretation of statistical output. Prepare to connect a question's design and outcome type to the appropriate method."]},"15:2":{"title":"Exam scope and what to bring","paragraphs":["The exam lasts ninety minutes, from 10:00 to 11:30 on Monday. The stated format is six to eight questions, with two or three on probability.","Bring two letter-size sheets with notes on both sides, a calculator, and the separate unannotated statistical tables. The listed reference tables are Normal, t, F, and chi-squared; tables will not be handed out.","The scope runs through Session 13 and excludes the Session 14 errors-and-power lecture. The review directs preparation toward the lecture material, homework, assigned reading, and relevant chapter questions."]},"15:3":{"title":"Population parameters describe the full group","paragraphs":["The two height populations have different centers and spreads. Population mean uses every individual's measurement, while population variance averages squared distances from that mean.","The symbols mu and sigma refer to population center and standard deviation. Variance is sigma squared and has squared measurement units.","When all population measurements are known, these are direct descriptions. Later sample statistics estimate these same quantities from a smaller observed subset."]},"15:4":{"title":"Sample statistics estimate population parameters","paragraphs":["The sample mean averages the measurements actually collected. The sample variance uses squared distances from that sample mean divided by sample size minus one.","The smaller denominator distinguishes the sample estimate from the full-population variance calculation. Taking the square root gives sample standard deviation in the original measurement units.","Random selection aims to avoid systematically favoring particular population members. This supports using the observed sample's center and spread as estimates for the larger population."]},"15:5":{"title":"Normal quantiles link positions with accumulated area","paragraphs":["The bell curve is centered at the population mean. The marked distances along the horizontal scale are measured in population standard deviations.","The cumulative probabilities below the axis describe how much area lies to the left of each position. A quantile reverses this reading by starting with a probability and locating its cutoff.","The central 95% boundaries leave matching small tails on either side. Distinguish those interval boundaries from the approximate two-standard-deviation rule when the question requires a precise table value."]},"15:6":{"title":"Sort before finding the median and interquartile range","paragraphs":["The median is the middle value of the ordered observations. For an even sample size, average the two central observations.","The range describes the extremes, while the interquartile range subtracts the 25th percentile from the 75th. It describes the width occupied by the middle half.","These summaries depend on positions in the sorted data. Locate the relevant observations before doing arithmetic, rather than using the original unsorted order."]},"15:7":{"title":"Interpolate a percentile in the ordered sample","paragraphs":["The rank rule finds where the requested percentile falls in the sorted list. Its integer part identifies the lower observation and its fractional part describes movement toward the next one.","Take that fraction of the difference between the adjacent observations and add it to the lower value. This estimates the measurement at the requested cumulative position.","Keep three objects visible: the rank, the selected pair of values, and the interpolated result. The rank is a location in the list, while the final percentile has the data's measurement units."]},"15:8":{"title":"Classify the outcome before choosing a method","paragraphs":["Continuous measurements can take values throughout a range. Discrete numerical observations count separate outcomes, such as episodes or affected joints.","Categorical observations identify group membership. Ordinal labels have a meaningful order, while nominal labels do not.","Look at what a difference between two values would mean. A numbered disease stage is an ordered category, whereas a count records how many events occurred; their numerical appearance alone does not make them the same data type."]},"15:9":{"title":"A random variable and one of its values","paragraphs":["The coded coin example uses the random variable \\(X\\) for an uncertain toss outcome. Lowercase \\(x\\) identifies a particular possible value of that variable.","Heads and tails receive numerical codes, but the codes do not change their probabilities. A fair coin gives each outcome probability one half.","A discrete variable places probability on separate allowed values. Before calculating, identify exactly what the variable records rather than assuming it describes every detail of the experiment."]},"15:10":{"title":"The probability function lists exact-value chances","paragraphs":["For each allowed value, the probability function gives the chance of observing exactly that value. The coded coin has separate masses at one and two.","The function is zero elsewhere because other codes cannot occur. Its graph therefore has isolated masses rather than a continuous band of probability.","To calculate an event involving several allowed values, add their masses. The function is the complete lookup rule from a possible outcome to its probability."]},"15:11":{"title":"Check the probability model before using it","paragraphs":["Each discrete probability must be nonnegative. The sum across the entire allowed set must equal one.","Those requirements ensure the function represents a complete distribution of chance. A missing probability can often be determined by subtracting the known total from one.","Once the table is valid, use its probabilities as weights in expectations and cumulative sums. Invalid weights would make later calculations lose their probability interpretation."]},"15:12":{"title":"A discrete CDF includes the cutoff value","paragraphs":["The distribution function accumulates the masses at all allowed values less than or equal to its argument. It includes a mass exactly at the cutoff.","The coded coin's CDF jumps to one half at the first value and to one at the second. Between allowed values, the accumulated amount stays constant.","The jump size equals the probability of that exact value. Read whether an inequality includes or excludes a boundary when translating a discrete probability question into cumulative terms."]},"15:13":{"title":"Expected value need not be an allowed outcome","paragraphs":["The fair coded coin has values one and two with equal weights. Their weighted average is 1.5, although no single toss produces that code.","An expectation describes the center of a distribution across repeated outcomes. It does not promise that the center itself is a possible observation.","For unequal probabilities, larger weights pull the center more strongly toward their values. Multiply each allowed value by its own probability before adding the contributions."]},"15:14":{"title":"Transform values before taking their expectation","paragraphs":["The function inside an expectation determines what is being averaged. Squared values require squaring each possible outcome before applying its probability weight.","Squared distances from the mean use a different transformed quantity. Subtract the mean first, then square and weight each result.","Averaging and transforming cannot usually be swapped. The expected square is generally different from the square of the expectation, which is why both appear in variance calculations."]},"15:15":{"title":"Variance is a weighted average of squared deviations","paragraphs":["Start with the distribution's mean and compare each possible outcome with it. Squaring the differences turns positive and negative departures into positive contributions.","Weight those squared distances by the original outcome probabilities and add them. The result is variance, measuring spread around the mean rather than around zero.","The displayed second-moment calculation supplies a shortcut to the same quantity. Keep expected squared value and squared expected value separate so that the subtraction has the correct meaning."]},"15:16":{"title":"A continuous density assigns probability through area","paragraphs":["For continuous heart rate, the probability between two boundaries is the density area over that interval. A curve height at one position is not itself that probability.","The density stays nonnegative and has total area one. Its shape describes which measurement ranges collect more probability per unit of width.","An integral accumulates those small area contributions. Use the requested interval as the integration range, with the density as the function being accumulated."]},"15:17":{"title":"Continuous expectations use density weights","paragraphs":["The expected measurement multiplies each possible value by its density and integrates over the allowed range. This is the continuous version of the discrete weighted sum.","For a function of the measurement, replace the value factor with that function while keeping the density weight. Squared values, linear transformations, and squared deviations all fit this pattern.","Decide what quantity is being averaged before integrating. The integration limits come from where the density is nonzero, rather than from a new range invented for the transformed expression."]},"15:18":{"title":"Build the CDF by accumulating density","paragraphs":["The cumulative function adds density area from the left up to a cutoff. It reports a probability and therefore stays between zero and one.","As the cutoff moves right, the accumulated area cannot decrease. Before the possible range it is zero, and after the full range it is one.","The density and cumulative function use different scales on their vertical axes. One describes probability per measurement interval; the other describes total probability accumulated so far."]},"15:19":{"title":"Convert CDF values into the requested region","paragraphs":["A left-tail probability comes directly from the cumulative function. A right-tail probability is the remaining area after subtracting that value from one.","An interval probability subtracts the lower cutoff's cumulative value from the upper cutoff's value. This removes everything lying below the requested interval.","The graphs make the subtraction visible. Mark the two boundaries on the density first, then use the cumulative values that retain exactly the selected area."]},"15:20":{"title":"The standard Normal curve is the reference scale","paragraphs":["The standard Normal variable has mean zero and standard deviation one. Its symmetric density places equal probability on matching sides of zero.","The cumulative function gives area to the left of a standard Normal value. At zero that area is one half.","Use the table for areas or reverse it for quantiles. The complicated-looking density expression defines the curve, while table lookup performs the course's practical probability calculations."]},"15:21":{"title":"Convert measurements to standard-deviation units","paragraphs":["Subtract the population mean from the measurement, then divide by population standard deviation. The result describes how far the measurement lies from the center relative to the distribution's width.","Negative standardized values fall below the mean and positive values above it. The original measurement units cancel.","To return a standardized cutoff to the original scale, multiply by the standard deviation and add the mean. These two directions support probability questions and inverse cutoff questions respectively."]},"15:22":{"title":"Standardize both boundaries of an interval","paragraphs":["The transformation preserves the probability of the selected event while changing its measurement scale. A Normal left-tail question therefore becomes a standard Normal left-tail question.","For an interval, convert both original boundaries with the same mean and standard deviation. Then subtract their cumulative standard Normal probabilities in upper-minus-lower order.","For a right tail, take the complement of the transformed cutoff's cumulative probability. The transformation changes the axis, while the requested side of the boundary still determines the area operation."]},"15:23":{"title":"Statistics have distributions across samples","paragraphs":["A statistic is calculated from observed sample data. Collecting a different sample generally gives a different mean, t value, or F value.","A sampling distribution describes that variation across all possible samples of a fixed design. Each point represents a completed sample's statistic.","This provides the reference for inference from one observed sample. Compare the obtained statistic with the results expected from repeated sampling under the population model being tested."]},"15:24":{"title":"Three samples estimate the same fixed population","paragraphs":["The population height mean and spread do not change across the illustration. Different groups of ten individuals nevertheless produce different sample means and standard deviations.","The sample mean estimates population center, and sample standard deviation estimates individual population spread. Neither estimate must equal its target in a particular sample.","The variation among these estimates motivates sampling distributions. Quantify how much an estimate can move because of selection alone before interpreting its discrepancy from a proposed population value."]},"15:25":{"title":"Collect the means of repeated samples of ten","paragraphs":["Every sample contributes one average to the plotted distribution. Its horizontal position depends on the combined heights of the ten selected individuals.","A sample containing more tall individuals can produce a high average, while one containing more short individuals can produce a low average. Repeated random selection produces the full range of such averages.","The plot's width concerns variation among averages. It is not the spread of the ten heights within any one sample."]},"15:26":{"title":"Compare individual height spread with mean spread","paragraphs":["The population distribution shows variation from one individual to another. The sample-mean distribution shows variation from one group average to another.","Both centers correspond to the same population mean. The averages cluster more narrowly because high and low individual deviations partly cancel.","Use individual standard deviation for questions about one individual's height. Use the standard error for questions about the mean of a specified number of independent heights."]},"15:27":{"title":"The central limit theorem concerns averages","paragraphs":["The sample mean has the population mean as its expected center. Its sampling standard deviation is the individual standard deviation divided by the square root of sample size.","For sufficiently large independent samples, the distribution of the mean is approximately Normal even if individual observations are not. The course gives thirty as its stated large-sample guideline.","The theorem changes the distribution available for describing the average; it does not change the population's original shape. Keep the word mean attached when using that Normal approximation."]},"15:28":{"title":"Skewed individuals can produce more symmetric averages","paragraphs":["The top distribution has a long right tail. A small sample can include an extreme individual, which can pull its average upward.","Averages of five remain visibly skewed, while averages of fifteen are more concentrated and less asymmetric. Their expected centers remain at the same population mean.","Increasing sample size affects both the shape and width of the mean distribution. The underlying individual population remains skewed even as its averages become more stable."]},"15:29":{"title":"Thirty and fifty observations improve the mean estimate","paragraphs":["The larger-sample averages cluster more tightly than the smaller-sample averages from the same skewed population. Their distributions also look more nearly bell shaped.","The common center remains 100 because increasing sample size does not alter the population mean being estimated. The standard error decreases as the square root of sample size increases.","For a probability about one of these averages, use its smaller sampling spread. Substituting the original individual spread would ignore the precision gained by averaging."]},"15:30":{"title":"Unbiasedness and precision of the sample mean","paragraphs":["The expectation of the sample mean equals the population mean. This means repeated sample means are correctly centered, the property called unbiasedness.","Its variance decreases with sample size when observations are independent. A smaller variance means estimates cluster more tightly across repeated samples.","The comparison with the median and the average of the extremes concerns a Normal population. In that setting the sample mean supplies a relatively stable estimator among the center estimates shown."]},"15:31":{"title":"Estimate SEM from a single observed sample","paragraphs":["The sample mean describes center and the sample variance describes spread among the collected individuals. These can both be calculated from one data set.","To estimate uncertainty in the mean, divide the sample standard deviation by the square root of sample size. This gives the estimated standard error of the mean.","Do not label the individual standard deviation as SEM. The separate variance-standard-error expression concerns uncertainty in the variance estimate, which is a different target from uncertainty in the mean."]},"15:32":{"title":"Recognize the ANOVA cell of the chooser","paragraphs":["The highlighted cell combines a numerical interval response with three or more independent groups. That design leads to the one-way ANOVA comparison of population means.","The response must remain a measured quantity, such as cardiac output or lung flow. Group membership identifies which mean each observation contributes to.","Read the design and response together before choosing F. A larger number of groups alone does not select ANOVA when the outcome is a category count."]},"15:33":{"title":"ANOVA begins with equal population means","paragraphs":["The null states that every group's population mean is equal. The alternative allows at least one pair to differ.","The displayed sample means and variances summarize each observed group. Means measure center, while variances measure scatter around each group's own center.","For a balanced design, the number of groups and common number of subjects per group define the next steps. Keep these two counts separate when constructing the variance estimates and degrees of freedom."]},"15:34":{"title":"Assemble balanced ANOVA's two variance estimates","paragraphs":["Average the group variances to estimate background individual variation. Average the equally weighted group means to find the grand mean.","Measure the scatter of those means around the grand mean. The squared quantity shown is variance among means, even though the nearby heading refers to standard error.","Multiply that mean variance by the common group size to obtain the between-groups estimate. It is now on the same individual-variance scale as the within-groups estimate."]},"15:35":{"title":"Complete the balanced ANOVA decision","paragraphs":["The F ratio places the between-groups estimate over the within-groups estimate. A large value indicates more group-center separation than expected from the observed background spread.","Use groups minus one for numerator degrees of freedom and the combined within-group contributions for denominator degrees of freedom. Both specify the F-table lookup.","Compare with the upper-tail critical value and state the result about population means. Rejecting the overall null establishes a difference somewhere, with particular pairs requiring follow-up comparisons."]},"15:36":{"title":"The professor narrows the ANOVA requirement","paragraphs":["The review explicitly excludes hand calculation using the sum-of-squares formulation. It also states that any midterm ANOVA problem will have equal group sizes.","That means the balanced variance-comparison calculation is the relevant hand method. Group means, standard deviations, and the common sample size provide its ingredients.","Practice averaging group variances, locating the grand mean, measuring variation among means, rescaling by group size, and obtaining F. This directly follows the announced design rather than adding an unequal-group derivation."]},"15:37":{"title":"Recognize the independent two-group mean test","paragraphs":["The highlighted cell selects an unpaired t test for a numerical response measured in two groups of different individuals. The comparison concerns their population means.","An unpaired design differs from measuring the same individuals before and after an intervention. The independent two-group denominator combines uncertainty from the two separately sampled groups.","Identify both group means, both spreads, and both sample sizes. These supply the observed difference and its standard error in the pooled procedure reviewed next."]},"15:38":{"title":"Set up the two-sample hypotheses and summaries","paragraphs":["The null equates the two population means. It does not require the two observed sample means to be exactly equal.","Each group supplies a sample mean and sample variance. The sample sizes can differ in this t-test formulation, so retain the correct size beside each group's summary.","Use the mean difference as the numerator signal. The variances and sizes then determine how uncertain that signal is under repeated independent sampling."]},"15:39":{"title":"Pool variance with degrees-of-freedom weights","paragraphs":["Multiply each sample variance by its group's sample size minus one. Add the contributions and divide by the combined sample sizes minus two.","That pooled variance estimates the common individual population variance assumed by this procedure. Use it in the standard error involving the two reciprocal sample sizes.","Divide the mean difference by that standard error to obtain t. Carry the same combined degrees of freedom into the reference table."]},"15:40":{"title":"Match the two-tailed t decision to its assumptions","paragraphs":["The pooled test assumes independent random samples, Normal populations, and equal population variances for the numerical outcome. These support the pooled estimate and t reference.","For a two-sided alternative, compare the absolute statistic with the critical magnitude. Positive and negative differences can both count as evidence.","A rejection means evidence against equal population means at the chosen level. A statistic below the threshold means the data do not establish that difference with this test."]},"15:41":{"title":"One group and a specified population mean","paragraphs":["The one-sample setting supplies measurements from one group and a fixed reference value. It does not estimate a second group's mean or variance.","Calculate the observed sample mean and sample variance. The null says the sampled population mean equals the reference.","The sample's estimated standard error provides the denominator for its discrepancy from that benchmark. This uses the same averaging principle as SEM, now inside an inference procedure."]},"15:42":{"title":"One-sample t uses sample size minus one","paragraphs":["Subtract the fixed reference mean from the sample mean. Divide by the sample standard deviation over the square root of sample size.","The resulting statistic uses one fewer degree of freedom than the number of observations. Select the corresponding two-tailed critical magnitude.","Compare magnitudes and interpret the result in the original measurement context. The numerator has measurement units, the denominator has the same units, and t is their dimensionless ratio."]},"15:43":{"title":"Pairwise t tests inherit ANOVA's error estimate","paragraphs":["After the overall ANOVA, each selected mean difference uses the common within-groups variance. This includes background information from all the original groups.","The reference degrees of freedom also come from that ANOVA error estimate. Do not replace them with degrees of freedom from a newly isolated two-group analysis.","The family of comparisons then needs Bonferroni or Holm thresholds. Estimating the standard error and controlling the multiple-comparison decisions are two separate parts of the follow-up procedure."]},"15:44":{"title":"Bonferroni uses one adjusted level for every pair","paragraphs":["Count the pairwise comparisons included in the family. Divide the chosen overall significance level by that count.","Every comparison uses this same adjusted level. More comparisons therefore require a more extreme statistic for individual rejection.","Use the ANOVA-based standard error and residual degrees of freedom to obtain each t statistic and critical value. Bonferroni changes the significance threshold while preserving the observed group differences."]},"15:45":{"title":"Holm uses ordered, successive thresholds","paragraphs":["Arrange the comparisons from strongest to weakest evidence. With a common t reference, this means descending absolute t magnitude.","Test the strongest using the overall level divided by the total number of comparisons. If it passes, continue with one fewer remaining comparison in the divisor.","Stop rejecting at the first failure. The later, larger significance levels cannot be applied independently after that stopping point; the ordered sequence is the defining part of Holm's procedure."]},"15:46":{"title":"Shift from measured means to nominal outcomes","paragraphs":["The highlighted cell moves into the nominal row of the chooser. The response is now category membership, such as event versus no event, rather than a continuous measurement.","A two-group proportion test examines whether the population event probabilities are equal. The same counts can also be arranged as a two-by-two contingency table.","Identify the event being counted and the denominator for each group. These determine the sample proportions before any pooled uncertainty or corrected statistic is calculated."]},"15:47":{"title":"A proportion is the mean of zero-one records","paragraphs":["Count the observations coded one and divide by the sample size. This is the sample estimate of the event probability.","The binary variance estimate uses that proportion and its complement. Dividing by sample size and taking the square root gives the estimated standard error of the proportion.","The individual outcome and its estimated rate have different spreads. Larger samples make the rate more precise even though each individual still has only the two possible outcomes."]},"15:48":{"title":"The proportion-test choice depends on the response","paragraphs":["The method chooser again highlights the two-group nominal comparison. Its repeated location reinforces that the same study-group count can lead to different methods depending on the outcome type.","Two independent groups with measured glucose use a mean test. Two independent groups classified by whether thrombi occurred use a proportion or contingency-table test.","Describe one person's recorded result before selecting the method. That quick check connects the table branch to the actual data rather than to superficial similarities in the wording of two questions."]},"15:49":{"title":"Assign event counts and denominators in the aspirin table","paragraphs":["The thrombus counts supply the event numerators, and the treatment totals supply the denominators. Placebo and aspirin have different group sizes.","The equal-proportion null concerns the population probabilities of thrombus formation. It does not say the raw event counts should match across unequal-sized groups.","Calculate both group proportions before pooling. Their difference describes the observed separation, while their combined event count estimates the shared null probability for the test denominator."]},"15:50":{"title":"Apply the required two-by-two correction","paragraphs":["The corrected z calculation uses the absolute sample-proportion gap minus the continuity adjustment. Its denominator uses a pooled event probability and both group sizes.","The course explicitly requires Yates's correction for these two-by-two comparisons. Keep the corrected numerator distinct from the original observed rate difference used to describe effect size.","Compare the resulting magnitude with a standard Normal critical value. The infinite-degrees-of-freedom row of the two-tailed t table supplies the same reference cutoffs."]},"15:51":{"title":"Contingency analysis extends the category comparison","paragraphs":["The marked cells include the nominal comparisons for two groups and for several independent groups. A contingency table cross-classifies group membership and outcome category.","The null predicts no association between those classifications. Expected counts allocate the pooled outcome pattern across the observed group sizes.","The next page expresses the mismatch through chi-squared. Use the table's dimensions to determine degrees of freedom and whether the course's two-by-two correction applies."]},"15:52":{"title":"General contingency-table calculation","paragraphs":["For each cell, multiply its row total by its column total and divide by the grand total. This supplies its expected count under independence.","Subtract expected from observed, square the discrepancy, divide by expected, and add across cells. For a two-by-two table, apply the course's continuity correction to the discrepancy first.","Degrees of freedom equal rows minus one times columns minus one. Compare the resulting statistic with the corresponding upper-tail chi-squared critical value to test the no-association null."]},"15:53":{"title":"Adjust a family of pairwise categorical comparisons","paragraphs":["The three pairwise two-by-two tables each produce a one-degree-of-freedom corrected chi-squared statistic. Order those statistics from largest to smallest for Holm's procedure.","The overall level is 0.01 in this displayed example. Successive thresholds divide it by three, then two, then one as long as earlier comparisons pass.","The two large comparisons pass, while the small remaining comparison does not. Keep each treatment-pair label attached throughout sorting and interpretation so that the rejection decisions map back to the correct groups."]},"15:54":{"title":"Goodness of fit compares one sample with target proportions","paragraphs":["Here the expected category distribution is specified in advance. Multiply each target probability by the observed sample total to obtain an expected count.","Add the squared observed-minus-expected discrepancies divided by their expected counts. With fixed target probabilities, the degrees of freedom are categories minus one.","This is a single-sample category-distribution question, not an independence question between two classifications. That distinction determines both where expected counts come from and which degrees of freedom are used."]},"15:55":{"title":"Apply goodness of fit to the exercise campaign","paragraphs":["The earlier survey gives the target proportions in three exercise categories. The current graduating class supplies the new observed counts and a total of 470 students.","Scale the earlier proportions to that current total before comparing. The expected counts represent what the current sample would look like if its category pattern had not changed.","The test combines excesses and shortages across all three categories. Its conclusion concerns whether the overall distribution differs from the benchmark, rather than a separate unadjusted test of each category."]},"15:56":{"title":"Study direction determines which rates are available","paragraphs":["A randomized trial assigns treatment before observing outcomes. A cohort study follows existing exposure groups forward without random assignment.","A case-control study instead selects people by outcome and looks backward for exposure. Its case and control totals reflect the sampling design.","These differences determine which denominators represent population risk. Forward follow-up supports event-risk comparisons, while case-control sampling naturally supports an exposure-odds ratio."]},"15:57":{"title":"Association measures occupy their own chooser branch","paragraphs":["The highlighted association cell asks how strongly two nominal variables are related. Relative risk and odds ratio provide effect-size descriptions for those category relationships.","A significance test and an association measure are not interchangeable. The former evaluates a null reference; the latter describes the observed relationship's magnitude and direction.","Use the study design to choose the measure, then define the event and reference group. This keeps a ratio's words synchronized with its numerator and denominator."]},"15:58":{"title":"Risk ratio and absolute reduction answer different questions","paragraphs":["For a trial or prospective cohort, calculate event probability within each exposure group. Relative risk divides the treatment probability by the control probability.","Absolute risk reduction subtracts the treatment event probability from the control event probability when treatment reduces an adverse outcome. It measures a difference on the probability scale.","A ratio near one indicates similar risks, while a small absolute difference means few additional events are changed per patient. Both descriptions can be useful for understanding the same table."]},"15:59":{"title":"Turn an absolute benefit into NNT","paragraphs":["The number needed to treat is the reciprocal of the absolute beneficial outcome difference. It expresses patients treated per additional favorable outcome compared with the reference condition.","A larger absolute benefit produces a smaller NNT. A very small benefit can produce a large NNT even when the relative reduction sounds impressive.","Convert percentages into decimal probabilities before taking the reciprocal. Retain precision through the calculation, and round upward when the answer must be a whole number of patients."]},"15:60":{"title":"Turn an absolute harm into NNH","paragraphs":["Number needed to harm uses the absolute increase in an adverse outcome. It reports how many patients would be treated or exposed per additional harmful event relative to the comparison group.","A smaller NNH indicates more frequent harm, while a larger NNH indicates less frequent additional harm. The interpretation runs in the opposite favorable direction from a small NNT.","The outcome label determines whether the absolute difference represents benefit or harm. Define that event first so the reciprocal receives the correct clinical name."]},"15:61":{"title":"Compare safety on the patient-count scale","paragraphs":["The repeated NNH definition emphasizes treatment safety. Translate a harmful rate increase into an expected additional event per number of people exposed.","Keep its reference condition and observation period attached. A harm estimate over one period is not automatically comparable with a benefit estimate over a different period.","When NNT and NNH refer to compatible comparisons, they put benefit and harm on a common patient-count scale. Their clinical importance still depends on what the beneficial and harmful outcomes actually are."]},"15:62":{"title":"Odds ratios work with case-control sampling","paragraphs":["Cases and controls are selected by their outcome status. Compare exposed-to-unexposed odds within cases with the same exposure odds within controls.","The cross product yields the odds ratio. One indicates equal odds, while values above or below one describe the association relative to the selected reference groups.","The same cross product can also be calculated from prospective data. Keep its interpretation in odds, and do not treat sampled case-control totals as direct population disease risks."]},"15:63":{"title":"What to understand in R functions and output","paragraphs":["The review distinguishes pnorm from qnorm: one returns cumulative probability for a cutoff, and the other returns a cutoff for a probability. The inputs and outputs run in opposite directions.","ANOVA output includes between-group and residual information, while t-test and chi-squared output provide their respective statistics, degrees of freedom, and p values. Interpret those values in the context of the stated test.","For an ANOVA follow-up, the residual mean square and residual degrees of freedom can supply the next calculation. Read the table's row labels rather than treating the output as a single p-value answer."]},"15:64":{"title":"Integrate the covered methods with the chooser","paragraphs":["The marked cells collect the course's main independent-group methods: t tests and ANOVA for numerical responses, proportion and contingency tests for nominal responses, and risk or odds measures for association.","The table is organized by design as well as measurement scale. Similar-looking data summaries can require different methods when one uses paired observations or category counts.","Use a short identification sequence: what was measured, which groups were sampled, and what population comparison is being asked? That leads from the study description to the appropriate calculation."]},"15:65":{"title":"Final preparation follows the announced exam rules","paragraphs":["The closing page repeats the ninety-minute Monday exam and the six-to-eight-question format. Probability accounts for two or three announced questions, so it needs practice alongside the inference methods.","The permitted notes are two double-sided letter sheets. Statistical tables must be brought separately without notes, and the calculator must be a standalone device rather than a phone.","The scope ends at Session 13, with the errors-and-power lecture excluded. Use the lecture, homework, reading, and review problems to rehearse selecting methods, showing calculations, consulting tables, and stating conclusions in context."]},"14:1":{"title":"Errors and power describe repeated test decisions","paragraphs":["A statistical test can make a wrong decision because different random samples produce different results. This lecture separates the two possible errors and asks how often a real effect is detected.","Power depends on the decision threshold, the size of the treatment effect, the amount of individual variation, and the sample size. Each factor changes how much the effect stands out from sampling noise.","The examples use t-test comparisons to connect these influences with power curves. This collection remains reference material outside the stated scope of the upcoming midterm."]},"14:2":{"title":"Categorical-analysis discussion and Homework 2","paragraphs":["The discussion revisits chi-squared methods for nominal data. An association analysis compares counts across groups, while goodness of fit compares one category distribution with specified expected probabilities.","The listed functions implement those different questions. Their output should be read alongside the observed counts, null hypothesis, and degrees of freedom.","Homework guidance connects the procedures to particular problems. Identify whether the input is a contingency table or a single category vector before deciding which expected-count calculation is appropriate."]},"14:3":{"title":"A real urine-production difference is detected","paragraphs":["The hypothetical drug population has a higher mean urine production than the placebo population. A real treatment difference therefore exists in the setup.","The selected samples produce a t magnitude that exceeds the displayed critical value. In this sample, the test rejects the no-effect null.","Because the underlying populations genuinely differ, that rejection is a correct detection. The sample means and spreads still fluctuate, so another pair of samples from the same populations can lead to a different decision."]},"14:4":{"title":"The same real effect can be missed","paragraphs":["The population drug effect is unchanged, but a different random sample produces a smaller standardized separation. The sample means and standard deviations differ from the previous example.","The observed t magnitude now lies below the critical value, so the test does not reject the no-effect null. In this hypothetical setup, that decision misses a known population difference.","A nonsignificant result can therefore arise even when treatment works. The size of the observed statistic depends on both the true effect and the sampling variation in the collected data."]},"14:5":{"title":"False positives and missed real effects","paragraphs":["The upper distribution represents repeated experiments when the drug has no effect. Its extreme tail contains false-positive rejections under the chosen rule.","The lower distribution represents repeated experiments when the drug truly increases urine production. Some results cross the rejection boundary, while others remain below it and miss that effect.","The displayed experiment counts estimate these two outcomes under the real-effect situation. Controlling false positives does not by itself determine how often the test detects a specified real difference."]},"14:6":{"title":"The four decision-and-reality combinations","paragraphs":["Reality has two possibilities: a treatment effect exists or it does not. The test also has two decisions: reject the no-effect null or do not reject it.","A Type I error rejects a true null, producing a false positive. A Type II error fails to reject when the specified effect is real, producing a false negative.","Power is the chance of correctly detecting that real effect. It is the complement of the Type II error probability and must be described for a particular effect size and study design."]},"14:7":{"title":"Power is the detected part of the real-effect distribution","paragraphs":["The lower distribution is generated when the drug really changes mean urine production. The shaded results beyond the rejection cutoff represent experiments that correctly detect it.","The unshaded results on the other side miss the effect. Both come from the same underlying treatment situation, with different samples accounting for the different decisions.","Power is the proportion of real-effect experiments that cross the boundary. It is not the probability that a particular significant result is true."]},"14:8":{"title":"Cholesterol: consequences of the two errors","paragraphs":["The null says these children's mean cholesterol matches the general childhood reference. The alternative says it is higher among children whose fathers died of heart disease.","A false positive would conclude that cholesterol is elevated when the population mean actually matches the reference. That could trigger unnecessary interventions, cost, worry, or side effects.","A false negative would miss a genuinely elevated population mean. Preventive action might then be withheld when it could have helped, linking the abstract error types with concrete clinical decisions."]},"14:9":{"title":"Birthweight: a different direction of concern","paragraphs":["The hospital sample is compared with a national mean birthweight. The alternative concerns a lower population mean in the low-socioeconomic-status setting.","A Type I error would identify a low-birthweight problem when the population mean is actually the same as the reference. A Type II error would miss a real reduction in birthweight.","The possible nursery decisions show why both errors matter. The labels depend on truth and the testing decision, rather than on whether the outcome is clinically described as higher or lower."]},"14:10":{"title":"A stricter significance threshold reduces detection","paragraphs":["The two comparisons use the same real treatment shift but different rejection thresholds. Reducing the significance level moves the cutoff farther into the null tail.","That reduces false-positive rejections when no effect exists. It also leaves less of the real-effect distribution beyond the cutoff, reducing power for the fixed effect and sample size.","The illustrated shift measures treatment separation relative to sampling uncertainty. Changing the threshold changes the decision boundary, while the underlying real-effect distribution stays in place."]},"14:11":{"title":"Larger effects are easier to detect","paragraphs":["The diagrams keep the background variation and group size fixed while changing the true treatment difference. A larger mean shift moves the real-effect statistic distribution farther from the null center.","More of that distribution then lies beyond the rejection cutoff, increasing power. A smaller effect leaves more overlap and produces more missed detections.","This is a relative-separation argument: the same measurement difference is easier to detect when it is large compared with uncertainty in the estimated mean difference."]},"14:12":{"title":"Read power as a function of effect size","paragraphs":["The horizontal axis describes the true increase in urine production, while the vertical axis describes the chance of rejecting the null for that increase. Sample size, individual spread, and significance level are fixed.","Near a zero effect, rejection occurs only at the false-positive rate. As the effect increases, the probability of crossing the boundary rises.","The curve links a proposed clinically relevant difference with expected detection. Choose the effect size first, then read the corresponding power for this particular design."]},"14:13":{"title":"Individual variability competes with the treatment signal","paragraphs":["The standardized shift divides the true mean difference by uncertainty in the estimated difference. Larger population spread enlarges that uncertainty when sample sizes are fixed.","The symbol delta names the true mean separation, and sigma names individual population standard deviation. Their ratio expresses effect size relative to background variation.","For equal-sized groups, the sample-size factor multiplies that standardized effect. The formula makes the cause-and-effect clear: more variability weakens separation, while more independent observations strengthen it."]},"14:14":{"title":"More subjects increase power","paragraphs":["The population effect and individual spread stay the same while each group grows from ten to twenty subjects. The standard error of the difference decreases.","The same true mean difference therefore becomes larger in standard-error units. More of the real-effect distribution crosses the rejection boundary, raising power.","Increasing sample size improves precision rather than changing the treatment's biological effect. Keep those two explanations separate when interpreting why the larger study detects the effect more often."]},"14:15":{"title":"Power rises along a sample-size curve","paragraphs":["The graph fixes a treatment difference, population spread, and significance level. Only the number of subjects per group changes along the horizontal axis.","Power increases as larger groups reduce sampling uncertainty. The curve rises rapidly in some regions and more slowly as it approaches a high detection probability.","Use it to connect a proposed group size with the chance of detecting that specified effect. The same curve cannot be reused unchanged if the effect or population variability changes."]},"14:16":{"title":"Standardized effect and sample size share a power graph","paragraphs":["The horizontal axis expresses the true mean difference relative to population standard deviation. Positive and negative differences both move away from the no-effect center in a two-sided test.","The separate curves represent different group sizes. A larger sample reaches high power for a smaller standardized effect because its mean difference is estimated more precisely.","The displayed ratio combines delta, sigma, and the equal group size into a standardized shift. Use the curve corresponding to the actual design rather than treating power as a function of effect size alone."]},"14:17":{"title":"A nonsignificant cardiac-index comparison revisited","paragraphs":["The anesthetic table returns to the cardiac-index means from halothane and morphine groups. The earlier t test did not reject equal population means.","Now the question changes: how capable was that small study of detecting a difference of the observed size? The group means alone do not answer that design question.","The sample standard deviations estimate individual variability, and the sample sizes specify the amount of information. These ingredients are used to locate an approximate power on the next page."]},"14:18":{"title":"Cardiac-index effect is small relative to spread","paragraphs":["The mean gap is 0.33 in cardiac-index units, and the pooled standard deviation is much larger. Their ratio gives a small standardized effect.","The slide uses the smaller group size as an approximate conservative input to the equal-size graph. At that effect and group size, the indicated power is low.","That means a real difference of this magnitude would often be missed by such a study. It explains why a nonsignificant result and a clinically interesting possible difference can coexist."]},"14:19":{"title":"Plan a sample size for the same cardiac-index effect","paragraphs":["Keep the proposed cardiac-index difference and estimated population spread fixed. This fixes the standardized effect on the graph's horizontal axis.","Now work backward from the desired power rather than reading power from an existing sample size. The diagram indicates a much larger equal group size than the original study used.","The displayed sample-size result belongs to that chosen effect, spread estimate, significance level, and power target. It is a planning calculation rather than a new test of the original observations."]},"14:20":{"title":"Plan detection of a percentage blood-pressure change","paragraphs":["The proposed effect is a quarter of the halothane group's mean blood pressure. Convert that percentage into an absolute difference in mmHg before standardizing it.","The pooled variance must be square-rooted to obtain the standard deviation used in the effect-to-spread ratio. Mixing variance with an unsquared mean difference would give incompatible units.","With the larger standardized effect, the graph indicates fewer subjects are needed for the same target power than in the cardiac-index example. The relative size of the signal explains that difference."]},"14:21":{"title":"Diets and a proposed cholesterol reduction","paragraphs":["The example asks about detecting a reduction in mean cholesterol from 190 to 165 mg/dL. The population standard deviation is estimated as 35 mg/dL.","The difference divided by that standard deviation gives a standardized effect near 0.71. The planned design has twenty people per diet group and uses the stated significance level.","Those are the inputs needed to read power from the graph. Keep the proposed mean difference separate from the observed variability; one supplies the signal and the other supplies the background spread."]},"14:22":{"title":"Read the cholesterol study's approximate power","paragraphs":["Locate the standardized effect near 0.71 on the horizontal axis. Move to the curve for twenty subjects per group, then read the corresponding vertical power.","The illustrated intersection is around 0.6. It describes a substantial chance of missing the proposed cholesterol difference even if that difference is real.","This is an approximate graph-based calculation. Its meaning is repeated-study detection under the stated assumptions, rather than the chance that the treatment helps one particular patient."]},"14:23":{"title":"Find the minimum effect for a target power","paragraphs":["Here group size stays at twenty, but the desired power is raised to 0.80. Work backward from that vertical value to the twenty-subject curve.","The graph indicates a standardized effect near 0.9. Multiply that ratio by the stated population standard deviation to return to cholesterol units.","The resulting detectable mean difference is about 31.5 mg/dL. A larger target difference is easier to detect at fixed sample size, so it can support a higher power than the smaller difference on the preceding page."]},"14:24":{"title":"Put the influences on power together","paragraphs":["Power is the chance of rejecting the no-effect null when a particular real effect exists. Its value depends on the test and the planned comparison.","A stricter significance level lowers power when everything else stays fixed. A larger effect relative to individual spread increases it, and a larger sample size increases precision and detection.","When population quantities are unknown, sample mean differences and pooled spread supply estimates for planning. Keep that estimation step visible rather than treating the sample's observed difference as a known population truth."]}}};
